{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b5e3e43fdc67c71a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n2\t\n3\tA preprint\n4\t\n5\tCross-cultural evaluation of taste–sound\n6\tcorrespondences in AI-generated music\n7\tMatteo Spanio\n8\t\n9\t∗1 , Massimiliano Zampini\n10\t\n11\tMesz\n12\t\n13\t4 , Masaki Ohno\n14\t\n15\t2 , Luisa Torri\n16\t\n17\t5 , Yuji Wada\n18\t\n19\t3 , Riccardo Migliavada\n20\t\n21\t5 , and Antonio Rodà\n22\t\n23\t3 , Bruno\n24\t\n25\t1\n26\t\n27\t1\n28\t\n29\tUniversity of Padova, Padova, Italy\n30\tUniversity of Trento, Trento, Italy\n31\t3\n32\tUniversity of Gastronomic Sciences, Pollenzo, Italy\n33\t4\n34\tUniversidad Nacional de Tres de Febrero, Buenos Aires, Argentina\n35\t5\n36\tRitsumeikan University, Kyoto, Japan\n37\t\n38\tarXiv:2608.03433v1 [cs.HC] 4 Aug 2026\n39\t\n40\t2\n41\t\n42\tAbstract\n43\tSonic seasoning research has shown that listeners attribute systematic gustatory and emotional\n44\tmeaning to sound, and text-to-music generative artificial intelligence (AI) has recently been\n45\tused to render gustatory prompts as musical stimuli. Whether the taste–sound correspondences\n46\tacquired by such models hold beyond the cultural context in which they were validated remains\n47\tuntested. We extended a single-country Italian study to a three-country online experiment\n48\tconducted in Argentina, Italy, and Japan (N = 361), cohorts selected to span three continents\n49\tand three distinct culinary and musical traditions. Participants first indicated their preference\n50\tbetween base and fine-tuned MusicGen excerpts generated from four taste prompts (sweet,\n51\tsour, bitter, salty), and then rated fine-tuned excerpts on twelve taste, emotion, and thermal\n52\tdescriptors. Preference for the fine-tuned model was confirmed in Argentina and Italy but\n53\tnot in Japan, and the salty prompt yielded the weakest correspondence in all three cohorts.\n54\tRatings differed substantially between countries, yet the main effect of country was no longer\n55\tdetectable once ratings had been standardized within participant, whereas the interactions\n56\tcharacterizing the mapping of prompts onto descriptors remained essentially unchanged. Much\n57\tof the apparent cross-cultural divergence is therefore attributable to differences in scale use;\n58\ta structural component nevertheless persists. Excerpts occupying the same acoustic region,\n59\tcharacterized by high spectral roughness and sensory dissonance, were predominantly labelled\n60\tsour in Italy and Japan but bitter in Argentina, and exploratory factor analysis indicated that the\n61\ttwelve descriptors were organized along different latent dimensions in each cohort. These results\n62\tindicate that cross-cultural variation in AI-mediated sonic seasoning operates at two levels: the\n63\toverall level at which taste is attributed to a given stimulus, and the relational structure of those\n64\tattributions. Evaluations of generative music systems across populations should accordingly\n65\tdistinguish response-style bias from genuine perceptual reorganization.\n66\t\n67\tKeywords: crossmodal correspondences · sonic seasoning · taste–sound associations · generative\n68\tAI · text-to-music · cross-cultural perception · response style\n69\t\n70\t∗\n71\t\n72\tCorresponding author: spanio@dei.unipd.it\n73\t\n74\t1\n75\t\n76\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n77\t\n78\t1\n79\t\n80\tA preprint\n81\t\n82\tIntroduction\n83\t\n84\tCrossmodal correspondences refer to the systematic associations that people consistently make\n85\tbetween features belonging to different sensory modalities, such as linking high-pitched sounds\n86\twith sweetness or low-pitched sounds with bitterness, despite the absence of any direct physical\n87\trelationship between them [1, 2]. These correspondences are thought to influence perceptual\n88\texpectations and multisensory integration, providing a theoretical basis for understanding how\n89\tinformation from one sensory modality can shape experience in another [1]. In this context, sonic\n90\tseasoning refers to the deliberate use of music or soundscapes to enhance or modify taste and\n91\tflavor perception by exploiting these auditory–gustatory correspondences [1–3]. Although growing\n92\tevidence suggests that sonic seasoning can reliably bias taste perception, recent work has also shown\n93\tthat these effects are partly mediated by the emotional qualities conveyed by music, highlighting\n94\tthe need to disentangle the respective contributions of crossmodal correspondences and affect [2, 3].\n95\tAt the same time, the apparent robustness of many crossmodal correspondences coexists with\n96\tgrowing evidence that their expression is shaped by cultural context [4–7]. Rather than being entirely\n97\tuniversal or entirely culture-specific, these correspondences arise from a combination of structural,\n98\tstatistical, semantic, and affective mechanisms, some of which are more susceptible to cultural\n99\tlearning than others [4]. Cross-cultural studies have shown that, although several musical and\n100\ttaste-related correspondences are consistently observed across populations, others vary according to\n101\tcultural background, reflecting differences in sensory experience, language, and learned associations\n102\trather than a simple East–West distinction [5, 6]. This issue is particularly relevant in the musical\n103\tdomain, where auditory–conceptual associations are shaped not only by structural regularities but\n104\talso by culturally acquired affective meanings [8]. Similarly, familiarity with a musical system and\n105\tlong-term enculturation influence both emotional interpretation and the semantic organization of\n106\tmusical stimuli across cultures [9, 10]. Consequently, identical musical stimuli may evoke different\n107\tsemantic and affective responses across cultures, potentially leading to different auditory–gustatory\n108\tcorrespondences [11].\n109\tThese developments have recently been extended to generative artificial intelligence (AI), where\n110\ttext-to-music models are used to translate gustatory prompts into musical stimuli and subsequently\n111\tevaluate whether listeners perceive the intended sensory qualities in the generated outputs [12, 13].\n112\tRather than functioning solely as creative systems, generative models have begun to serve as\n113\texperimental tools for investigating whether computational models can learn and reproduce human\n114\tauditory–gustatory correspondences and, generally speaking, cross-modal patterns [14]. Spanio\n115\tet al. [12] fine-tuned a MusicGEN model and evaluated its outputs through two complementary\n116\texperiments: a model-comparison task assessing whether fine-tuning improved taste coherence\n117\tand a semantic-rating task measuring gustatory, emotional, and thermal responses elicited by\n118\tthe generated music. Using a different approach, Deng et al. [13] combined professional sound\n119\tdesigners with a human-in-the-loop text-to-audio generation pipeline to create taste-congruent\n120\tsoundscapes. Although the two studies adopted substantially different generation strategies and\n121\tevaluation procedures, participants in both were able to reliably recover taste-related information\n122\tfrom AI-generated auditory stimuli. Together, these findings demonstrate that generative AI\n123\tprovides a controlled and reproducible experimental platform for investigating auditory–gustatory\n124\tcorrespondences. In particular, the methodology introduced by Spanio et al. makes it possible\n125\tto generate standardized musical stimuli from identical gustatory prompts and evaluate them\n126\tsystematically using shared semantic scales across different groups of listeners.\n127\tDespite these promising findings, the extent to which AI-mediated auditory–gustatory correspondences generalize across cultures remains unknown. Although the fine-tuned MusicGEN model\n128\toutperformed its non-fine-tuned counterpart in taste-matching judgments [12], the original validation\n129\t2\n130\t\n131\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n132\t\n133\tA preprint\n134\t\n135\trelied on a relatively homogeneous participant sample and therefore could not determine whether\n136\tthe learned correspondences extend beyond the cultural context in which they were acquired. This\n137\tlimitation is reinforced by recent work on culturally adaptive music representation learning, which\n138\targues that current foundation models remain strongly influenced by predominantly Western training\n139\tdata and cannot be assumed to transfer uniformly across musical traditions [15]. Consequently,\n140\tif generative models learn auditory–gustatory correspondences from culturally situated data, it\n141\tremains unclear whether they capture broadly shared perceptual regularities or instead reproduce\n142\tculturally specific semantic and affective associations.\n143\tA second challenge concerns measurement. Cross-cultural comparisons based on semantic rating\n144\tscales may reflect not only genuine perceptual differences but also systematic differences in response\n145\tstyle, including acquiescence and extreme responding [16–18]. Interpreting cross-cultural variation\n146\ttherefore requires distinguishing differences in perception from differences in the way participants\n147\tuse rating scales.\n148\tThis study investigates whether AI-mediated sonic seasoning generalizes across culturally distinct\n149\tpopulations and, if not, what accounts for the observed differences. Specifically, we examine whether\n150\tthe superiority of a fine-tuned model over its non-fine-tuned counterpart can be replicated beyond\n151\tthe cultural context in which it was originally validated, whether listeners from different countries\n152\torganize auditory–gustatory correspondences in similar ways, and whether any observed crosscultural variation reflects genuine differences in perceptual organization, culturally specific semantic\n153\tassociations, response styles, or a combination of these factors.\n154\tTo address these questions, we conducted a cross-cultural online experiment involving participants\n155\tfrom Argentina, Italy, and Japan. These countries were selected to capture diversity in both culinary\n156\tand musical traditions while maintaining a balanced three-country comparison. All three have\n157\twell-established culinary traditions in which food plays an important cultural and social role. Italian\n158\tcooking and Japanese washoku are recognized by UNESCO as elements of the Intangible Cultural\n159\tHeritage of Humanity [19, 20], while studies of Argentine gastronomy likewise identify food as a\n160\tcentral component of cultural identity and social life [21]. Because the experimental stimuli consist\n161\tof taste prompts, cultural differences in the representation and interpretation of basic tastes are\n162\tdirectly relevant to the way listeners may associate taste with sound.\n163\tThe three countries also differ substantially in their musical traditions. Italy serves as the\n164\treference condition because the present study directly extends the original validation conducted\n165\tthere [12]. Japan provides a culturally distant comparison in which musical enculturation has been\n166\tshown to influence the perception of emotional meaning in music [10]. Argentina contributes a\n167\tdistinct Western perspective, combining a rich musical tradition with an established body of research\n168\ton taste–music correspondences [22]. Including Argentina also makes it possible to distinguish\n169\tdifferences associated with broader cultural distance from those that may emerge between culturally\n170\tdistinct Western populations, avoiding a comparison restricted to Europe and East Asia alone.\n171\tTaken together, this design allows us to evaluate whether AI-mediated auditory–gustatory\n172\tcorrespondences remain stable across culturally diverse populations while separating three sources\n173\tof variation that are often conflated in cross-cultural studies: overall preference for the generated\n174\tstimuli, culturally specific response styles, and differences in the organization of auditory–gustatory\n175\tcorrespondences themselves. Figure 1 provides an overview of the study design.\n176\t\n177\t3\n178\t\n179\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n180\t\n181\tA preprint\n182\t\n183\tFig 1. Conceptual overview of the study. AI-generated music—fine-tuned MusicGen clips for four taste\n184\tprompts (sweet, sour, bitter, salty)—is evaluated in two parallel ways and the results are compared. Human\n185\tlisteners in three countries (Japan, Argentina, and Italy) rate the clips through an online survey, yielding\n186\tsubjective taste, emotion, and thermal judgments, while an acoustic feature analysis (librosa) characterizes\n187\tthe same clips on descriptors such as brightness, roughness, and dissonance. Comparing the two asks\n188\twhether listeners from different cultures hear the same taste in the same sound.\n189\t\n190\t2\n191\t\n192\tMaterials and methods\n193\t\n194\t2.1\n195\t\n196\tParticipants\n197\t\n198\tThe study included three national cohorts recruited independently in Italy, Japan, and Argentina.\n199\tThe Italian cohort combined respondents from the original Italian dataset of Spanio et al. [12],\n200\trestricted to participants who self-identified as ethnically Italian, with an additional Italian cohort\n201\trecruited subsequently using the same questionnaire, auditory stimuli, and experimental protocol.\n202\tThe Japanese and Argentine cohorts were recruited specifically for the present study. Because the\n203\tpreviously collected and newly recruited Italian participants completed an identical experimental\n204\tprocedure, their responses were pooled for all subsequent analyses. After excluding incomplete\n205\tquestionnaires, the final sample comprised N = 361 participants (Italy: n = 117, Japan: n = 140,\n206\tArgentina: n = 104). An additional English-language cohort was also collected but was excluded\n207\tfrom the present analyses because its culturally heterogeneous composition was not compatible\n208\twith the three-country comparison. The study protocol and questionnaire were approved by the\n209\t4\n210\t\n211\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n212\t\n213\tA preprint\n214\t\n215\tEthics Committee of the University of Gastronomic Sciences of Pollenzo (UNISG Ethics Committee,\n216\tMinutes 19122025). The study was conducted in accordance with the ethical principles of the\n217\tDeclaration of Helsinki and its later amendments (October 2024). Participation was voluntary\n218\tand restricted to adults (18 years or older). Before beginning the survey, all participants provided\n219\tinformed consent electronically through PsyToolkit [23]. No personally identifying information was\n220\tcollected, and all analyses were performed on anonymized data\n221\t\n222\t2.2\n223\t\n224\tMaterials and stimuli\n225\t\n226\tThe present study employed the same auditory stimuli and experimental materials used by Spanio et\n227\tal. [12], allowing a direct cross-cultural replication of the original Italian experiment. The stimulus\n228\tset consisted of short musical excerpts generated with the MusicGen text-to-music model [24]\n229\tusing two versions of the model: the original public model and the fine-tuned model proposed by\n230\tSpanio et al. Each model generated four musical excerpts corresponding to the taste prompts sweet,\n231\tsour, bitter, and salty. All prompts were specified in English, the language of MusicGen’s T5 text\n232\tencoder. No modifications were made to the original stimuli or experimental protocol. Consequently,\n233\tresponses from the original Italian dataset were directly comparable with those collected from\n234\tthe newly recruited Italian, Japanese, and Argentine participants, allowing all observations to be\n235\tanalyzed within a common experimental framework (see Section 2.1). For each participant, the\n236\tassignment of prompts to audio clips, the presentation order of the clips within each task, and the\n237\torder of the experimental trials were independently randomized.\n238\t\n239\t2.3\n240\t\n241\tProcedure\n242\t\n243\tThe experiment was administered online using PsyToolkit [23]. The questionnaire was available in\n244\tfour languages (Italian, Japanese, Spanish, and English), and participants completed the survey in\n245\ttheir native language whenever possible. The English version was administered exclusively to the\n246\tinternational cohort, which was excluded from the present analyses (see Section 2.1). Apart from\n247\ttranslation, all versions of the questionnaire were identical in structure, stimuli, response scales, and\n248\texperimental flow. The survey comprised a demographic questionnaire followed by two listening\n249\ttasks. The first task (Model Preference) assessed participants’ preference between musical excerpts\n250\tgenerated by the original and fine-tuned MusicGen models: on each of five trials participants heard\n251\tone clip from each model, both generated from the same taste prompt, and placed their preference\n252\ton a 0–10 slider anchored at the two clips, with 5 as the neutral midpoint. Responses are reported\n253\tthroughout so that values above 5 indicate preference for the fine-tuned model. The second task\n254\t(Semantic Rating) required participants to evaluate the same musical excerpts on twelve 1–5 rating\n255\tscales covering gustatory, emotional, and thermal dimensions. We refer to these twelve rating\n256\titems collectively as descriptors: four taste descriptors (sweet, sour, bitter, salty), six emotion\n257\tdescriptors (happy, sad, anger, disgust, fear, surprise), and two thermal descriptors (hot, cold).\n258\tThroughout the paper, prompt is reserved for the taste used to condition the generative model,\n259\tso that the input given to MusicGen and the descriptor rated by participants are never confused\n260\teven when the two coincide. Prompt-to-audio assignment, clip order within each task, and task\n261\torder were independently randomized for every participant. Sample size was determined through\n262\tthe Monte Carlo power analysis reported in Appendix A. Based on the preliminary Italian dataset,\n263\tapproximately 80 participants per country were estimated to provide 80% statistical power to detect\n264\tthe expected medium-sized effect of prompt. All three cohorts exceeded this target. The demographic\n265\tquestionnaire collected participants’ age, gender, self-reported ethnicity, country of origin, country\n266\tof residence, self-reported musical expertise (hearing experience), and self-reported food expertise\n267\t5\n268\t\n269\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n270\t\n271\tA preprint\n272\t\n273\t(eating experience). Country names entered as free text were harmonized to standardized English\n274\tlabels before analysis.\n275\t\n276\t2.4\n277\t\n278\tStatistical analysis\n279\t\n280\tAll analyses were carried out in R [25]. The complete analysis code (data loading, models, figures, and\n281\ttables) is openly available at https://github.com/matteospanio/multimodal-survey-analysis,\n282\tand the survey data and audio stimuli are archived on Zenodo (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.20841611).\n283\t2.4.1\n284\t\n285\tDemographic equivalence\n286\t\n287\tWe first tested whether the independently recruited cohorts were comparable on background variables.\n288\tAge was compared with a Kruskal–Wallis test; gender, hearing experience, and eating experience with\n289\tFisher’s exact tests on the contingency tables (Monte Carlo simulation for sparse cells). Variables\n290\tthat differed across groups were carried into the perception-rating models as nuisance covariates.\n291\t2.4.2\n292\t\n293\tTask 1: model preference\n294\t\n295\tBecause the five trials are nested within participants, the unit of analysis is the participant: we\n296\taveraged each participant’s trials (overall and within each taste prompt shown) and ran all inferential\n297\ttests on those means. The participant means were non-normal (Shapiro–Wilk p < .001), so the tests\n298\twere non-parametric. Within each country we tested H0 : µ = 5 against H1 : µ > 5 with a Wilcoxon\n299\tsigned-rank test, where values above 5 indicate preference for the fine-tuned model, reporting\n300\tthe rank-biserial correlation r (a non-parametric effect size) with a bootstrap 95% confidence\n301\tinterval. Between-country differences used a Kruskal–Wallis H test (pooled and per prompt; the\n302\tper-prompt tests in Appendix A (Table A5) are uncorrected), reporting the H-based η 2 ; significant\n303\tomnibus effects were followed by Dunn’s pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction [26].\n304\tWithin-country per-prompt Wilcoxon tests (Bonferroni-corrected) identified which prompts drove\n305\tthe pooled preference. As a robustness check on the trial-level data, we additionally fitted linear\n306\tmixed models with random intercepts for participant and stimulus, both within each country (against\n307\tthe neutral point) and across countries (fixed effect of country).\n308\t2.4.3\n309\t\n310\tTask 2: perception ratings\n311\t\n312\tFor the rating task, the 1–5 value was the dependent variable, with data in long format (one\n313\t(participant, prompt, descriptor) triple per observation). Following [12], we restricted the model to\n314\trespondents reporting Male or Female gender and not classified as Professional Eaters (too sparse\n315\tfor reliable inference).\n316\tThe main fixed-effects model was:\n317\tvalue ∼ group × prompt × descriptor + hearing_experience + eating_experience + gender.\n318\tWe estimated Type III sums of squares with car::Anova, using sum-to-zero contrasts so that\n319\tlower-order terms remain interpretable under the three-way interaction. Residual heteroscedasticity\n320\twas present but tolerable at this sample size [27–29]. The full ANOVA tables, including sums of\n321\tsquares, are given in Appendix A (Tables A7 and A8); we also ran per-country ANOVAs without\n322\tthe group factor (Table A9).\n323\tBecause each participant contributed 36 nested ratings and several participants rated the same\n324\texcerpt, we also fitted a linear mixed model with the same fixed-effects structure and crossed\n325\trandom intercepts for participant and stimulus song, (1 | participant) + (1 | song), as a participant6\n326\t\n327\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n328\t\n329\tA preprint\n330\t\n331\tand stimulus-level robustness check. We retain the fixed-effects ANOVA as the main table for\n332\tcomparability with the earlier Italian study but treat an ANOVA result as supported only when the\n333\tmixed model agrees.\n334\tWe further checked the cross-cultural conclusion by re-fitting the omnibus model and comparing\n335\tthe four group-related terms (i) retaining the respondents excluded above (gender other than Male\n336\tor Female, and Professional Eaters) and (ii) excluding the legacy Italian wave, and by fitting an\n337\tItalian-only mixed model testing collection wave and its interaction with the prompt × descriptor\n338\tstructure.\n339\t2.4.4\n340\t\n341\tResponse-style diagnostics and sensitivity analysis\n342\t\n343\tResponse styles—using the scale in a particular way independently of item content—can confound\n344\tcross-cultural ratings [16–18], so we computed two participant-level diagnostics: the Acquiescence\n345\tResponse Style index (ARS), the mean of each participant’s 36 ratings, and the Extreme Response\n346\tStyle index (ERS), the proportion of those ratings equal to 1 or 5. Both were compared across\n347\tgroups with Kruskal–Wallis tests; the indices and a complementary variance-homogeneity (Levene)\n348\tcheck are tabulated in Appendix A (Tables A13–A15).\n349\tWe then re-fitted the omnibus model on within-subject z-scored ratings (each rating minus the\n350\tparticipant’s mean over all 36 ratings, divided by the within-participant standard deviation; one\n351\tparticipant with zero within-subject variance was excluded here only). Because this normalization\n352\tremoves between-participant location and scale by design, the informative terms are group ×\n353\tdescriptor and group × prompt × descriptor, which capture changes in the structure of the mapping\n354\trather than in overall scale use.\n355\t2.4.5\n356\t\n357\tInter-annotator agreement\n358\t\n359\tFor each (song, taste-descriptor) cell we treated its raters as annotators and computed two agreement\n360\tindices on the 1–5 Likert rescaled to [0, 1]: the mean pairwise distance (MPD), the average |xi − xj |\n361\tbetween annotators (smaller is better, 0 indicating perfect agreement), and Krippendorff’s α with\n362\tthe interval metric δ 2 (a, b) = (a − b)2 . Because α is invariant to a common linear transform, it is\n363\tidentical on raw or within-country z-scored ratings; we also computed an ordinal-metric variant\n364\tas a sensitivity check. Per cohort, α was aggregated over the four taste descriptors by pooling the\n365\tmatching song–descriptor cells (100 songs, 25 per prompt; cells with fewer than two raters dropped);\n366\tthe per-cohort values are tabulated in Appendix A (Table A16).\n367\t2.4.6\n368\t\n369\tFactor structure comparison\n370\t\n371\tThe twelve descriptor ratings were submitted to exploratory factor analysis (maximum-likelihood\n372\textraction, oblimin rotation) with the psych package [30], the number of factors chosen by parallel\n373\tanalysis per cohort and for the pooled sample (reported in Appendix A, Figs A4–A6). To compare\n374\tstructures across countries, we computed Tucker’s congruence coefficients [31] between all pairs\n375\tof group-specific solutions, truncating each comparison to the smaller number of factors retained;\n376\tvalues above 0.95 indicate factor equivalence and values in [0.85, 0.95) fair similarity. We treated\n377\tthe solutions as exploratory: the Italian three-factor solution returned a Heywood case for the\n378\tsweet item (loading near 1.00, residual variance near zero), so the Italian structure is a descriptive\n379\tsummary of how the descriptor space partitions in that cohort, not a stable measurement model.\n380\t\n381\t7\n382\t\n383\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n384\t\n385\t2.4.7\n386\t\n387\tA preprint\n388\t\n389\tAcoustic characterization\n390\t\n391\tTo test whether low-level acoustic cues underlie the cross-cultural effects, we characterized the\n392\t100 fine-tuned clips (25 per prompt) with the eight descriptors used by Deng et al.—spectral\n393\tcentroid (brightness), bandwidth (frequency content), rolloff (sharpness), fundamental frequency\n394\t(pitch), spectral flux (roughness), onset strength (articulation, attack salience), tempo (speed), and\n395\tzero-crossing rate (discontinuity, noisiness)—computed with librosa [32], plus a Plomp–Levelt\n396\tsensory-dissonance index in the parametrization of Sethares [33]. Following Deng et al., we read\n397\tthese as relative patterns across prompts rather than absolute thresholds.\n398\t\n399\t3\n400\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d14d750d-13e3-418b-be1b-cfe55ef8a9ec", "uuid": "db9d87d2-2e26-4339-b6c6-805cc405bd58", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T06:02:04.779Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "Taste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nCross-cultural evaluation of taste–sound\ncorrespondences in AI-generated music\nMatteo Spanio\n\n∗1 , Massimiliano Zampini\n\nMesz\n\n4 , Masaki Ohno\n\n2 , Luisa Torri\n\n5 , Yuji Wada\n\n3 , Riccardo Migliavada\n\n5 , and Antonio Rodà\n\n3 , Bruno\n\n1\n\n1\n\nUniversity of Padova, Padova, Italy\nUniversity of Trento, Trento, Italy\n3\nUniversity of Gastronomic Sciences, Pollenzo, Italy\n4\nUniversidad Nacional de Tres de Febrero, Buenos Aires, Argentina\n5\nRitsumeikan University, Kyoto, Japan\n\narXiv:2608.03433v1 [cs.HC] 4 Aug 2026\n\n2\n\nAbstract\nSonic seasoning research has shown that listeners attribute systematic gustatory and emotional\nmeaning to sound, and text-to-music generative artificial intelligence (AI) has recently been\nused to render gustatory prompts as musical stimuli. Whether the taste–sound correspondences\nacquired by such models hold beyond the cultural context in which they were validated remains\nuntested. We extended a single-country Italian study to a three-country online experiment\nconducted in Argentina, Italy, and Japan (N = 361), cohorts selected to span three continents\nand three distinct culinary and musical traditions. Participants first indicated their preference\nbetween base and fine-tuned MusicGen excerpts generated from four taste prompts (sweet,\nsour, bitter, salty), and then rated fine-tuned excerpts on twelve taste, emotion, and thermal\ndescriptors. Preference for the fine-tuned model was confirmed in Argentina and Italy but\nnot in Japan, and the salty prompt yielded the weakest correspondence in all three cohorts.\nRatings differed substantially between countries, yet the main effect of country was no longer\ndetectable once ratings had been standardized within participant, whereas the interactions\ncharacterizing the mapping of prompts onto descriptors remained essentially unchanged. Much\nof the apparent cross-cultural divergence is therefore attributable to differences in scale use;\na structural component nevertheless persists. Excerpts occupying the same acoustic region,\ncharacterized by high spectral roughness and sensory dissonance, were predominantly labelled\nsour in Italy and Japan but bitter in Argentina, and exploratory factor analysis indicated that the\ntwelve descriptors were organized along different latent dimensions in each cohort. These results\nindicate that cross-cultural variation in AI-mediated sonic seasoning operates at two levels: the\noverall level at which taste is attributed to a given stimulus, and the relational structure of those\nattributions. Evaluations of generative music systems across populations should accordingly\ndistinguish response-style bias from genuine perceptual reorganization.\n\nKeywords: crossmodal correspondences · sonic seasoning · taste–sound associations · generative\nAI · text-to-music · cross-cultural perception · response style\n\n∗\n\nCorresponding author: spanio@dei.unipd.it\n\n1\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\n1\n\nA preprint\n\nIntroduction\n\nCrossmodal correspondences refer to the systematic associations that people consistently make\nbetween features belonging to different sensory modalities, such as linking high-pitched sounds\nwith sweetness or low-pitched sounds with bitterness, despite the absence of any direct physical\nrelationship between them [1, 2]. These correspondences are thought to influence perceptual\nexpectations and multisensory integration, providing a theoretical basis for understanding how\ninformation from one sensory modality can shape experience in another [1]. In this context, sonic\nseasoning refers to the deliberate use of music or soundscapes to enhance or modify taste and\nflavor perception by exploiting these auditory–gustatory correspondences [1–3]. Although growing\nevidence suggests that sonic seasoning can reliably bias taste perception, recent work has also shown\nthat these effects are partly mediated by the emotional qualities conveyed by music, highlighting\nthe need to disentangle the respective contributions of crossmodal correspondences and affect [2, 3].\nAt the same time, the apparent robustness of many crossmodal correspondences coexists with\ngrowing evidence that their expression is shaped by cultural context [4–7]. Rather than being entirely\nuniversal or entirely culture-specific, these correspondences arise from a combination of structural,\nstatistical, semantic, and affective mechanisms, some of which are more susceptible to cultural\nlearning than others [4]. Cross-cultural studies have shown that, although several musical and\ntaste-related correspondences are consistently observed across populations, others vary according to\ncultural background, reflecting differences in sensory experience, language, and learned associations\nrather than a simple East–West distinction [5, 6]. This issue is particularly relevant in the musical\ndomain, where auditory–conceptual associations are shaped not only by structural regularities but\nalso by culturally acquired affective meanings [8]. Similarly, familiarity with a musical system and\nlong-term enculturation influence both emotional interpretation and the semantic organization of\nmusical stimuli across cultures [9, 10]. Consequently, identical musical stimuli may evoke different\nsemantic and affective responses across cultures, potentially leading to different auditory–gustatory\ncorrespondences [11].\nThese developments have recently been extended to generative artificial intelligence (AI), where\ntext-to-music models are used to translate gustatory prompts into musical stimuli and subsequently\nevaluate whether listeners perceive the intended sensory qualities in the generated outputs [12, 13].\nRather than functioning solely as creative systems, generative models have begun to serve as\nexperimental tools for investigating whether computational models can learn and reproduce human\nauditory–gustatory correspondences and, generally speaking, cross-modal patterns [14]. Spanio\net al. [12] fine-tuned a MusicGEN model and evaluated its outputs through two complementary\nexperiments: a model-comparison task assessing whether fine-tuning improved taste coherence\nand a semantic-rating task measuring gustatory, emotional, and thermal responses elicited by\nthe generated music. Using a different approach, Deng et al. [13] combined professional sound\ndesigners with a human-in-the-loop text-to-audio generation pipeline to create taste-congruent\nsoundscapes. Although the two studies adopted substantially different generation strategies and\nevaluation procedures, participants in both were able to reliably recover taste-related information\nfrom AI-generated auditory stimuli. Together, these findings demonstrate that generative AI\nprovides a controlled and reproducible experimental platform for investigating auditory–gustatory\ncorrespondences. In particular, the methodology introduced by Spanio et al. makes it possible\nto generate standardized musical stimuli from identical gustatory prompts and evaluate them\nsystematically using shared semantic scales across different groups of listeners.\nDespite these promising findings, the extent to which AI-mediated auditory–gustatory correspondences generalize across cultures remains unknown. Although the fine-tuned MusicGEN model\noutperformed its non-fine-tuned counterpart in taste-matching judgments [12], the original validation\n2\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nrelied on a relatively homogeneous participant sample and therefore could not determine whether\nthe learned correspondences extend beyond the cultural context in which they were acquired. This\nlimitation is reinforced by recent work on culturally adaptive music representation learning, which\nargues that current foundation models remain strongly influenced by predominantly Western training\ndata and cannot be assumed to transfer uniformly across musical traditions [15]. Consequently,\nif generative models learn auditory–gustatory correspondences from culturally situated data, it\nremains unclear whether they capture broadly shared perceptual regularities or instead reproduce\nculturally specific semantic and affective associations.\nA second challenge concerns measurement. Cross-cultural comparisons based on semantic rating\nscales may reflect not only genuine perceptual differences but also systematic differences in response\nstyle, including acquiescence and extreme responding [16–18]. Interpreting cross-cultural variation\ntherefore requires distinguishing differences in perception from differences in the way participants\nuse rating scales.\nThis study investigates whether AI-mediated sonic seasoning generalizes across culturally distinct\npopulations and, if not, what accounts for the observed differences. Specifically, we examine whether\nthe superiority of a fine-tuned model over its non-fine-tuned counterpart can be replicated beyond\nthe cultural context in which it was originally validated, whether listeners from different countries\norganize auditory–gustatory correspondences in similar ways, and whether any observed crosscultural variation reflects genuine differences in perceptual organization, culturally specific semantic\nassociations, response styles, or a combination of these factors.\nTo address these questions, we conducted a cross-cultural online experiment involving participants\nfrom Argentina, Italy, and Japan. These countries were selected to capture diversity in both culinary\nand musical traditions while maintaining a balanced three-country comparison. All three have\nwell-established culinary traditions in which food plays an important cultural and social role. Italian\ncooking and Japanese washoku are recognized by UNESCO as elements of the Intangible Cultural\nHeritage of Humanity [19, 20], while studies of Argentine gastronomy likewise identify food as a\ncentral component of cultural identity and social life [21]. Because the experimental stimuli consist\nof taste prompts, cultural differences in the representation and interpretation of basic tastes are\ndirectly relevant to the way listeners may associate taste with sound.\nThe three countries also differ substantially in their musical traditions. Italy serves as the\nreference condition because the present study directly extends the original validation conducted\nthere [12]. Japan provides a culturally distant comparison in which musical enculturation has been\nshown to influence the perception of emotional meaning in music [10]. Argentina contributes a\ndistinct Western perspective, combining a rich musical tradition with an established body of research\non taste–music correspondences [22]. Including Argentina also makes it possible to distinguish\ndifferences associated with broader cultural distance from those that may emerge between culturally\ndistinct Western populations, avoiding a comparison restricted to Europe and East Asia alone.\nTaken together, this design allows us to evaluate whether AI-mediated auditory–gustatory\ncorrespondences remain stable across culturally diverse populations while separating three sources\nof variation that are often conflated in cross-cultural studies: overall preference for the generated\nstimuli, culturally specific response styles, and differences in the organization of auditory–gustatory\ncorrespondences themselves. Figure 1 provides an overview of the study design.\n\n3\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nFig 1. Conceptual overview of the study. AI-generated music—fine-tuned MusicGen clips for four taste\nprompts (sweet, sour, bitter, salty)—is evaluated in two parallel ways and the results are compared. Human\nlisteners in three countries (Japan, Argentina, and Italy) rate the clips through an online survey, yielding\nsubjective taste, emotion, and thermal judgments, while an acoustic feature analysis (librosa) characterizes\nthe same clips on descriptors such as brightness, roughness, and dissonance. Comparing the two asks\nwhether listeners from different cultures hear the same taste in the same sound.\n\n2\n\nMaterials and methods\n\n2.1\n\nParticipants\n\nThe study included three national cohorts recruited independently in Italy, Japan, and Argentina.\nThe Italian cohort combined respondents from the original Italian dataset of Spanio et al. [12],\nrestricted to participants who self-identified as ethnically Italian, with an additional Italian cohort\nrecruited subsequently using the same questionnaire, auditory stimuli, and experimental protocol.\nThe Japanese and Argentine cohorts were recruited specifically for the present study. Because the\npreviously collected and newly recruited Italian participants completed an identical experimental\nprocedure, their responses were pooled for all subsequent analyses. After excluding incomplete\nquestionnaires, the final sample comprised N = 361 participants (Italy: n = 117, Japan: n = 140,\nArgentina: n = 104). An additional English-language cohort was also collected but was excluded\nfrom the present analyses because its culturally heterogeneous composition was not compatible\nwith the three-country comparison. The study protocol and questionnaire were approved by the\n4\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nEthics Committee of the University of Gastronomic Sciences of Pollenzo (UNISG Ethics Committee,\nMinutes 19122025). The study was conducted in accordance with the ethical principles of the\nDeclaration of Helsinki and its later amendments (October 2024). Participation was voluntary\nand restricted to adults (18 years or older). Before beginning the survey, all participants provided\ninformed consent electronically through PsyToolkit [23]. No personally identifying information was\ncollected, and all analyses were performed on anonymized data\n\n2.2\n\nMaterials and stimuli\n\nThe present study employed the same auditory stimuli and experimental materials used by Spanio et\nal. [12], allowing a direct cross-cultural replication of the original Italian experiment. The stimulus\nset consisted of short musical excerpts generated with the MusicGen text-to-music model [24]\nusing two versions of the model: the original public model and the fine-tuned model proposed by\nSpanio et al. Each model generated four musical excerpts corresponding to the taste prompts sweet,\nsour, bitter, and salty. All prompts were specified in English, the language of MusicGen’s T5 text\nencoder. No modifications were made to the original stimuli or experimental protocol. Consequently,\nresponses from the original Italian dataset were directly comparable with those collected from\nthe newly recruited Italian, Japanese, and Argentine participants, allowing all observations to be\nanalyzed within a common experimental framework (see Section 2.1). For each participant, the\nassignment of prompts to audio clips, the presentation order of the clips within each task, and the\norder of the experimental trials were independently randomized.\n\n2.3\n\nProcedure\n\nThe experiment was administered online using PsyToolkit [23]. The questionnaire was available in\nfour languages (Italian, Japanese, Spanish, and English), and participants completed the survey in\ntheir native language whenever possible. The English version was administered exclusively to the\ninternational cohort, which was excluded from the present analyses (see Section 2.1). Apart from\ntranslation, all versions of the questionnaire were identical in structure, stimuli, response scales, and\nexperimental flow. The survey comprised a demographic questionnaire followed by two listening\ntasks. The first task (Model Preference) assessed participants’ preference between musical excerpts\ngenerated by the original and fine-tuned MusicGen models: on each of five trials participants heard\none clip from each model, both generated from the same taste prompt, and placed their preference\non a 0–10 slider anchored at the two clips, with 5 as the neutral midpoint. Responses are reported\nthroughout so that values above 5 indicate preference for the fine-tuned model. The second task\n(Semantic Rating) required participants to evaluate the same musical excerpts on twelve 1–5 rating\nscales covering gustatory, emotional, and thermal dimensions. We refer to these twelve rating\nitems collectively as descriptors: four taste descriptors (sweet, sour, bitter, salty), six emotion\ndescriptors (happy, sad, anger, disgust, fear, surprise), and two thermal descriptors (hot, cold).\nThroughout the paper, prompt is reserved for the taste used to condition the generative model,\nso that the input given to MusicGen and the descriptor rated by participants are never confused\neven when the two coincide. Prompt-to-audio assignment, clip order within each task, and task\norder were independently randomized for every participant. Sample size was determined through\nthe Monte Carlo power analysis reported in Appendix A. Based on the preliminary Italian dataset,\napproximately 80 participants per country were estimated to provide 80% statistical power to detect\nthe expected medium-sized effect of prompt. All three cohorts exceeded this target. The demographic\nquestionnaire collected participants’ age, gender, self-reported ethnicity, country of origin, country\nof residence, self-reported musical expertise (hearing experience), and self-reported food expertise\n5\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\n(eating experience). Country names entered as free text were harmonized to standardized English\nlabels before analysis.\n\n2.4\n\nStatistical analysis\n\nAll analyses were carried out in R [25]. The complete analysis code (data loading, models, figures, and\ntables) is openly available at https://github.com/matteospanio/multimodal-survey-analysis,\nand the survey data and audio stimuli are archived on Zenodo (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.20841611).\n2.4.1\n\nDemographic equivalence\n\nWe first tested whether the independently recruited cohorts were comparable on background variables.\nAge was compared with a Kruskal–Wallis test; gender, hearing experience, and eating experience with\nFisher’s exact tests on the contingency tables (Monte Carlo simulation for sparse cells). Variables\nthat differed across groups were carried into the perception-rating models as nuisance covariates.\n2.4.2\n\nTask 1: model preference\n\nBecause the five trials are nested within participants, the unit of analysis is the participant: we\naveraged each participant’s trials (overall and within each taste prompt shown) and ran all inferential\ntests on those means. The participant means were non-normal (Shapiro–Wilk p < .001), so the tests\nwere non-parametric. Within each country we tested H0 : µ = 5 against H1 : µ > 5 with a Wilcoxon\nsigned-rank test, where values above 5 indicate preference for the fine-tuned model, reporting\nthe rank-biserial correlation r (a non-parametric effect size) with a bootstrap 95% confidence\ninterval. Between-country differences used a Kruskal–Wallis H test (pooled and per prompt; the\nper-prompt tests in Appendix A (Table A5) are uncorrected), reporting the H-based η 2 ; significant\nomnibus effects were followed by Dunn’s pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction [26].\nWithin-country per-prompt Wilcoxon tests (Bonferroni-corrected) identified which prompts drove\nthe pooled preference. As a robustness check on the trial-level data, we additionally fitted linear\nmixed models with random intercepts for participant and stimulus, both within each country (against\nthe neutral point) and across countries (fixed effect of country).\n2.4.3\n\nTask 2: perception ratings\n\nFor the rating task, the 1–5 value was the dependent variable, with data in long format (one\n(participant, prompt, descriptor) triple per observation). Following [12], we restricted the model to\nrespondents reporting Male or Female gender and not classified as Professional Eaters (too sparse\nfor reliable inference).\nThe main fixed-effects model was:\nvalue ∼ group × prompt × descriptor + hearing_experience + eating_experience + gender.\nWe estimated Type III sums of squares with car::Anova, using sum-to-zero contrasts so that\nlower-order terms remain interpretable under the three-way interaction. Residual heteroscedasticity\nwas present but tolerable at this sample size [27–29]. The full ANOVA tables, including sums of\nsquares, are given in Appendix A (Tables A7 and A8); we also ran per-country ANOVAs without\nthe group factor (Table A9).\nBecause each participant contributed 36 nested ratings and several participants rated the same\nexcerpt, we also fitted a linear mixed model with the same fixed-effects structure and crossed\nrandom intercepts for participant and stimulus song, (1 | participant) + (1 | song), as a participant6\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nand stimulus-level robustness check. We retain the fixed-effects ANOVA as the main table for\ncomparability with the earlier Italian study but treat an ANOVA result as supported only when the\nmixed model agrees.\nWe further checked the cross-cultural conclusion by re-fitting the omnibus model and comparing\nthe four group-related terms (i) retaining the respondents excluded above (gender other than Male\nor Female, and Professional Eaters) and (ii) excluding the legacy Italian wave, and by fitting an\nItalian-only mixed model testing collection wave and its interaction with the prompt × descriptor\nstructure.\n2.4.4\n\nResponse-style diagnostics and sensitivity analysis\n\nResponse styles—using the scale in a particular way independently of item content—can confound\ncross-cultural ratings [16–18], so we computed two participant-level diagnostics: the Acquiescence\nResponse Style index (ARS), the mean of each participant’s 36 ratings, and the Extreme Response\nStyle index (ERS), the proportion of those ratings equal to 1 or 5. Both were compared across\ngroups with Kruskal–Wallis tests; the indices and a complementary variance-homogeneity (Levene)\ncheck are tabulated in Appendix A (Tables A13–A15).\nWe then re-fitted the omnibus model on within-subject z-scored ratings (each rating minus the\nparticipant’s mean over all 36 ratings, divided by the within-participant standard deviation; one\nparticipant with zero within-subject variance was excluded here only). Because this normalization\nremoves between-participant location and scale by design, the informative terms are group ×\ndescriptor and group × prompt × descriptor, which capture changes in the structure of the mapping\nrather than in overall scale use.\n2.4.5\n\nInter-annotator agreement\n\nFor each (song, taste-descriptor) cell we treated its raters as annotators and computed two agreement\nindices on the 1–5 Likert rescaled to [0, 1]: the mean pairwise distance (MPD), the average |xi − xj |\nbetween annotators (smaller is better, 0 indicating perfect agreement), and Krippendorff’s α with\nthe interval metric δ 2 (a, b) = (a − b)2 . Because α is invariant to a common linear transform, it is\nidentical on raw or within-country z-scored ratings; we also computed an ordinal-metric variant\nas a sensitivity check. Per cohort, α was aggregated over the four taste descriptors by pooling the\nmatching song–descriptor cells (100 songs, 25 per prompt; cells with fewer than two raters dropped);\nthe per-cohort values are tabulated in Appendix A (Table A16).\n2.4.6\n\nFactor structure comparison\n\nThe twelve descriptor ratings were submitted to exploratory factor analysis (maximum-likelihood\nextraction, oblimin rotation) with the psych package [30], the number of factors chosen by parallel\nanalysis per cohort and for the pooled sample (reported in Appendix A, Figs A4–A6). To compare\nstructures across countries, we computed Tucker’s congruence coefficients [31] between all pairs\nof group-specific solutions, truncating each comparison to the smaller number of factors retained;\nvalues above 0.95 indicate factor equivalence and values in [0.85, 0.95) fair similarity. We treated\nthe solutions as exploratory: the Italian three-factor solution returned a Heywood case for the\nsweet item (loading near 1.00, residual variance near zero), so the Italian structure is a descriptive\nsummary of how the descriptor space partitions in that cohort, not a stable measurement model.\n\n7\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\n2.4.7\n\nA preprint\n\nAcoustic characterization\n\nTo test whether low-level acoustic cues underlie the cross-cultural effects, we characterized the\n100 fine-tuned clips (25 per prompt) with the eight descriptors used by Deng et al.—spectral\ncentroid (brightness), bandwidth (frequency content), rolloff (sharpness), fundamental frequency\n(pitch), spectral flux (roughness), onset strength (articulation, attack salience), tempo (speed), and\nzero-crossing rate (discontinuity, noisiness)—computed with librosa [32], plus a Plomp–Levelt\nsensory-dissonance index in the parametrization of Sethares [33]. Following Deng et al., we read\nthese as relative patterns across prompts rather than absolute thresholds.\n\n3\n", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 3184}}}
