{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9c7ba8fefb22fe7b", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\t\n401\tResults\n402\t\n403\tThe analyses below follow a chain of increasingly specific questions. We first verify that the three\n404\tcohorts are comparable on background variables, and then ask whether the fine-tuned model is\n405\tpreferred over the base model in each country (Task 1). Turning to the rating task (Task 2), we\n406\task whether the three cohorts describe the same clips in the same way, and whether any country\n407\tdifference reflects how participants use the rating scale or how they organize the sound-to-taste\n408\tmapping itself. We then characterize that organization: how much listeners within a cohort agree\n409\twith one another, and how the twelve descriptors group into a smaller number of latent dimensions.\n410\tFinally, we turn from the listeners to the stimuli and ask what the generated clips actually sound\n411\tlike, and which taste descriptor each cohort attaches to a given acoustic profile. Throughout, prompt\n412\tdenotes the taste used to condition the generative model and descriptor one of the twelve items\n413\tparticipants rated.\n414\t\n415\t3.1\n416\t\n417\tSample composition\n418\t\n419\tThe pooled sample comprises 140 Japanese, 104 Argentine, and 117 Italian respondents (Table 1)\n420\tand differs substantially on background variables: age varied by group (H(2) = 77.89, p < .001), and\n421\tFisher’s exact tests rejected homogeneity for gender, hearing experience, and eating experience (all\n422\tp < .001 via Monte Carlo simulation). The cohorts are therefore not interchangeable on background\n423\tcharacteristics, and these variables are carried as covariates in every rating model reported below.\n424\tTable 1. Demographic summary by cultural group. Age is mean ± standard deviation; gender is percentage\n425\tof the within-group sample (Other / NA pools Other and Not specified); time is the median minutes to\n426\tcomplete the survey (median because completion time is right-skewed).\n427\t\n428\t3.2\n429\t\n430\tGroup\n431\t\n432\tN\n433\t\n434\tAge (y)\n435\t\n436\t% Male\n437\t\n438\t% Female\n439\t\n440\t% Other / NA\n441\t\n442\tTime (min)\n443\t\n444\tJapan\n445\tArgentina\n446\tItaly\n447\t\n448\t140\n449\t104\n450\t117\n451\t\n452\t27.2 ± 13.3\n453\t42.4 ± 15.0\n454\t34.2 ± 14.7\n455\t\n456\t28.6\n457\t49.0\n458\t53.8\n459\t\n460\t68.6\n461\t50.0\n462\t43.6\n463\t\n464\t2.9\n465\t1.0\n466\t2.6\n467\t\n468\t8.0\n469\t8.0\n470\t8.0\n471\t\n472\tPooled\n473\t\n474\t361\n475\t\n476\t33.9 ± 15.5\n477\t\n478\t42.7\n479\t\n480\t55.1\n481\t\n482\t2.2\n483\t\n484\t8.0\n485\t\n486\tTask 1: model preference\n487\t\n488\tTask 1 asks the simplest question in the study: presented with two clips generated from the same\n489\ttaste prompt, do listeners prefer the one produced by the fine-tuned model? Preferences are placed\n490\ton a 0–10 slider with 5 as the neutral midpoint, so a participant mean above 5 favours the fine-tuned\n491\tmodel and a mean below 5 the base model.\n492\t8\n493\t\n494\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n495\t\n496\tA preprint\n497\t\n498\tThe answer is a clear partial replication. One-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the participant\n499\tmean scores show that the fine-tuned model is preferred in Argentina (median = 5.6, rank-biserial\n500\tr = .29, 95% CI [.08, .51], p = .006) and in Italy (median = 5.2, r = .30, [.09, .50], p = .003), but not\n501\tin Japan (median = 5.0, r = −.15, [−.34, .05], p = .93). The within-country effect sizes are small\n502\tin Argentina and Italy and indistinguishable from zero in Japan. Comparing the three countries\n503\tdirectly, the participant means differ, H(2) = 11.47, p = .003, η 2 = .03; Dunn’s post-hoc tests\n504\tattribute this to the two contrasts involving Japan, which differs from both Argentina (padj = .006)\n505\tand Italy (padj = .006), whereas Argentina and Italy do not differ (padj = 1.00). A linear mixed\n506\tmodel on the trial-level data, with random intercepts for participant and stimulus, reproduces this\n507\tpattern: the estimated preference above neutral is positive in Argentina (+0.43, p = .012) and Italy\n508\t(+0.47, p = .021) and null in Japan (−0.15, p = .33), with a significant country effect (p = .003).\n509\tThe conclusion therefore does not depend on how the nesting of trials within participants is handled.\n510\tFig 2 shows where the advantage comes from. Within Argentina and Italy the per-prompt\n511\tbreakdown locates the fine-tuning advantage in the sweet and sour prompts (Bonferroni-corrected\n512\tr in [.34, .60], all padj ≤ .010), and the between-country difference is concentrated on the same\n513\ttwo prompts (sweet H(2) = 16.45, p < .001; sour H(2) = 13.37, p = .001), where Argentine and\n514\tItalian medians are highest and Japanese medians stay near neutral. Bitter and salty show no\n515\treliable cross-country difference (p = .094 and p = .216, uncorrected; full test table in Appendix A,\n516\tTable A5). Salty is also the weakest matched prompt overall—at the neutral point in Argentina\n517\t(r = −.16) and below neutral in Italy (r = −.46) and Japan (r = −.35). Because it fails in every\n518\tcohort alike, this points to a limitation of the fine-tuned model’s salty stimuli rather than to a\n519\tcountry-specific failure, a reading we return to when we examine the acoustics of the clips themselves.\n520\t\n521\tFig 2. Model preference summaries by prompt and cultural group, shown as one panel per cohort. Points\n522\tmark the median of the participant mean scores, vertical bars show bootstrap 95% confidence intervals, and\n523\tthe dashed horizontal line marks the neutral point of 5. Scores in the shaded band (above 5) favor the\n524\tfine-tuned model, those below favor the base model.\n525\t\n526\t3.3\n527\t\n528\tTask 2: perception ratings\n529\t\n530\tTask 1 establishes that the three cohorts reach different verdicts on the model. Task 2 asks how\n531\tthey hear the clips, by having every participant rate the same fine-tuned excerpts on all twelve\n532\t\n533\t9\n534\t\n535\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n536\t\n537\tA preprint\n538\t\n539\tdescriptors. The question is whether the three-dimensional pattern of ratings—country by prompt\n540\tby descriptor—has the same shape everywhere.\n541\tThe left half of Table 2 reports the omnibus Type III ANOVA on the raw ratings. The largest\n542\tterm is the main effect of group (F (2, 11,768) = 436.22, p < .001), that is, a difference in the overall\n543\tlevel at which the three cohorts rate. The critical result, however, is the three-way group × prompt\n544\t× descriptor interaction (F (66, 11,768) = 2.96, p < .001): this term asks whether the profile of\n545\tdescriptor ratings across the four prompts has the same shape in all three countries, and it says\n546\tthat it does not. The two-way group × descriptor interaction is also sizeable (F (22, 11,768) = 14.43,\n547\tp < .001), while the raw group × prompt term is small (F (6, 11,768) = 2.43, p = .024). All of\n548\tthese cross-cultural terms are highly significant but account for little variance individually (all\n549\tgroup-related partial η 2 ≤ .03), so they are best described as structured but modest tendencies\n550\trather than wholesale reorganizations of perception.\n551\tTwo checks confirm that this structure is not an artifact of how the data were modelled or\n552\tassembled. First, a linear mixed model with the same fixed-effects structure and crossed random\n553\tintercepts for participant and stimulus song, (1 | participant) + (1 | song), leaves the three-way\n554\tinteraction highly significant (p < .001) and confirms the group, prompt, descriptor, and group ×\n555\tdescriptor effects; only the raw group × prompt interaction fails to survive (p = .428). We therefore\n556\ttreat the three-way interaction, not the prompt-level country shift, as the stable cross-cultural\n557\tsignal. Song-level variance is small relative to the residual (σ̂song = 0.04 versus σ̂residual = 1.01), so\n558\tindividual songs are controlled for without driving the result. Second, retaining the eight respondents\n559\twith a gender other than Male or Female and the twenty-two Professional Eaters excluded by the\n560\tmain model, or excluding the legacy Italian wave, leaves the four group-related terms essentially\n561\tunchanged, with the group × descriptor and three-way interactions highly significant (p < .001)\n562\tin every variant. Within the Italian cohort, collection wave has no effect on ratings (p = .81) and\n563\tno interaction with the prompt × descriptor structure (p = .28), so the legacy (n = 90) and newly\n564\tcollected (n = 27) Italian participants are interchangeable here; the legacy-excluded re-fit, resting\n565\ton only those 27, is best read as a directional check rather than a precise re-estimate.\n566\tA group main effect of this size is, however, ambiguous. It could mean that Japanese listeners\n567\tgenuinely heard more taste in the clips, or simply that they used the 1–5 scale differently from the\n568\tother two cohorts. The next section separates these two possibilities.\n569\t\n570\t3.4\n571\t\n572\tSeparating scale use from perceptual structure\n573\t\n574\tResponse styles—habits of using a rating scale in a particular way regardless of item content—\n575\tdiffer markedly between the cohorts (Fig 3). Japanese participants have a substantially higher\n576\tacquiescence index, the mean of their 36 ratings (ARS = 2.69), than Argentine (1.98) or Italian\n577\t(1.97) participants, but a lower extreme-response index, the proportion of ratings at either end of the\n578\tscale (Japan = 0.26, Argentina = 0.48, Italy = 0.47); Kruskal–Wallis tests confirm both differences\n579\t(ARS: p = 6.84 × 10−35 ; ERS: p = 8.61 × 10−18 ). In short, Japanese respondents used higher but\n580\tmore central ratings, the Argentine and Italian groups lower and more extreme ones—exactly the\n581\tprofile that would inflate a group main effect without any difference in what was heard.\n582\tTo remove this component we re-expressed every rating relative to the participant’s own mean\n583\tand spread (within-subject z-scoring), which places a participant who rates everything high on the\n584\tsame footing as one who rates everything low. The right half of Table 2 shows the consequence,\n585\tand it is sharp. The main effect of group disappears completely (F (2, 11,732) = 0.002, p = .998,\n586\tpartial η 2 < .001), and the group × prompt term disappears with it (F (6, 11,732) = 0.61, p = .723).\n587\tBy contrast, group × descriptor and the three-way interaction remain strong, with effect sizes\n588\tessentially unchanged from the raw model (partial η 2 = .028 and .018). The corresponding z-scored\n589\t10\n590\t\n591\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n592\t\n593\tA preprint\n594\t\n595\tTable 2. Type III ANOVA of the Task 2 ratings, before (left) and after (right) within-subject normalization,\n596\twith sum-to-zero contrasts on all categorical predictors. Numerator df is the same in both models;\n597\tdenominator df = 11,768 (raw) and 11,732 (z-scored, one participant with zero within-subject variance\n598\texcluded). Reading the two halves side by side isolates what response style accounts for: the group main\n599\teffect collapses from partial η 2 = .069 to below .001 and group × prompt becomes non-significant, whereas\n600\tgroup × descriptor and the three-way interaction are essentially unchanged. Full tables including sums of\n601\tsquares are given in Appendix A (Tables A7 and A8).\n602\tRaw ratings\n603\tTerm\n604\t\n605\tdf\n606\t\n607\tGroup\n608\tPrompt\n609\tDescriptor\n610\tHearing experience\n611\tEating experience\n612\tGender\n613\tGroup × Prompt\n614\tGroup × Descriptor\n615\tPrompt × Descriptor\n616\tGroup × Prompt × Descriptor\n617\t\n618\t2 436.22 < .001\n619\t3 23.28 < .001\n620\t11 29.64 < .001\n621\t2 31.62 < .001\n622\t1\n623\t3.55\n624\t.060\n625\t1\n626\t0.91\n627\t.340\n628\t6\n629\t2.43\n630\t.024\n631\t22 14.43 < .001\n632\t33 25.58 < .001\n633\t66\n634\t2.96 < .001\n635\t\n636\tWithin-subject z scores\n637\t\n638\tp partial η 2\n639\t\n640\tF\n641\t\n642\t.069\n643\t.006\n644\t.027\n645\t.005\n646\t< .001\n647\t< .001\n648\t.001\n649\t.026\n650\t.067\n651\t.016\n652\t\n653\tF\n654\t0.002\n655\t11.51\n656\t35.44\n657\t0.03\n658\t0.08\n659\t0.01\n660\t0.61\n661\t15.54\n662\t28.41\n663\t3.18\n664\t\n665\tp partial η 2\n666\t.998\n667\t< .001\n668\t< .001\n669\t.969\n670\t.779\n671\t.911\n672\t.723\n673\t< .001\n674\t< .001\n675\t< .001\n676\t\n677\t< .001\n678\t.003\n679\t.032\n680\t< .001\n681\t< .001\n682\t< .001\n683\t< .001\n684\t.028\n685\t.074\n686\t.018\n687\t\n688\tmixed model (1 | participant) + (1 | song) gives the same qualitative result (non-significant group\n689\t× prompt, p = .714; significant three-way interaction, p < .001). Most of the raw country-level\n690\trating shift was therefore a scale-use difference; what remains is the country-specific structure of the\n691\tmapping.\n692\tThat surviving structure is visible directly in the taste ratings (Fig 4). Each panel is a promptby-descriptor grid, and a model that reproduced the intended correspondences would place its\n693\thighest values along the outlined diagonal. Italy comes closest, retaining a clear sweet–sour–bitter\n694\tdiagonal with a weak salty cell. Argentina keeps sweet and salty but cross-maps sour and bitter,\n695\tthe sour prompt pulling the bitter descriptor and the bitter prompt the sour one. Japan is the\n696\tmost diffuse, with off-diagonal spillover around sour and no salty match. The raw ratings show the\n697\tsame three arrangements superimposed on the country-level offset (Appendix A, Fig A1), which\n698\tis what motivates working with the normalized values. The differences extend into the emotional\n699\tand thermal descriptors, again not identically. In the raw ratings, sweet clips are rated happier and\n700\twarmer and sour and bitter clips colder and more negative, especially in Argentina and Italy, while\n701\tthe Japanese cohort shows a uniformly elevated affective profile across both positive and negative\n702\tdescriptors (Appendix A, Fig A2). Once that inflation is removed a structural difference remains\n703\t(Fig A3): the sour prompt recruits negative-affect descriptors (anger, disgust, fear) most strongly in\n704\tJapan, the bitter prompt aligns chiefly with sadness in both Japan and Italy, and the sweet prompt\n705\taligns with happy and hot in all three cohorts but to different degrees.\n706\t\n707\t3.5\n708\t\n709\tAgreement between listeners\n710\t\n711\tThe heatmaps average over listeners, so a cohort could show a clean mean structure while its\n712\tindividual members disagreed completely. Before asking how the descriptor space is organized, we\n713\ttherefore ask how much the listeners within a cohort agree about any single clip, using the mean\n714\tpairwise distance (MPD, the average absolute difference between two raters of the same cell, 0\n715\tindicating perfect agreement) and Krippendorff’s α (1 perfect agreement, 0 chance).\n716\tCell-level disagreement is moderate and comparable across cohorts (mean MPD 0.27–0.29 on\n717\t11\n718\t\n719\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n720\t\n721\tA preprint\n722\t\n723\tFig 3. Per-participant response-style distributions by cultural group. The left panel shows the acquiescence\n724\tresponse style index (participant mean across ratings), and the right panel shows the extreme response style\n725\tindex (proportion of 1s and 5s); the two panels use independent y-axes because the indices differ in unit.\n726\tJapanese participants show the highest acquiescence but the lowest extreme response, whereas the Argentine\n727\tand Italian cohorts use lower but more extreme ratings.\n728\t\n729\tFig 4. Mean within-subject z-scored taste ratings in the Task 2 ANOVA sample. Rows are the prompt used\n730\tto generate the clip and columns the taste descriptor rated; black outlines mark the matching\n731\tprompt–descriptor cells, where a faithful correspondence would produce the highest values.\n732\t\n733\t12\n734\t\n735\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n736\t\n737\tA preprint\n738\t\n739\ta 0–1 scale). The aggregated α values are all well below the conventional 0.667 threshold, as\n740\texpected for single-trial perceptual ratings of AI-generated audio by untrained listeners, but differ\n741\tmeaningfully across cohorts: Argentina (α = 0.192) and Japan (α = 0.172) reach roughly twice the\n742\tagreement of Italy (α = 0.103); an ordinal-metric variant falls within ±0.01 of these (cohort table in\n743\tAppendix A, Table A16; the per-cell breakdown is archived with the data). Italy is the noisiest\n744\tmainly because its bitter cell collapses to chance: Italian listeners rated the bitter prompt almost\n745\tidentically on all four taste descriptors (Appendix A, Fig A1), leaving little systematic signal for\n746\traters to agree on. Agreement is thus low everywhere and lowest in the cohort with the cleanest\n747\taverage diagonal, so the cross-cultural differences reported here are group-level regularities that\n748\temerge from noisy individual judgments rather than consensus perceptions.\n749\t\n750\t3.6\n751\t\n752\tFactor structure across cultures\n753\t\n754\tIf the three cohorts organize the descriptor space differently, that difference should appear not only\n755\tin the mean ratings but in how the twelve descriptors covary. Exploratory factor analysis groups\n756\tdescriptors that move together into a small number of latent dimensions, and Tucker’s congruence\n757\tcoefficient measures how similar two such solutions are (1.0 identical; above 0.95 conventionally\n758\ttreated as equivalent, [0.85, 0.95) as fair similarity).\n759\tParallel analysis returns four factors for Japan, three for Argentina, and four for Italy. When\n760\tthe solutions are truncated to three factors for comparison, congruence remains low: 0.777 for\n761\tJapan–Italy, 0.613 for Japan–Argentina, and 0.460 for Argentina–Italy (Table 3), all below the\n762\tequivalence threshold and two of the three below the lower bound of fair similarity. A participant\n763\tbootstrap returns wide intervals whose upper bounds still fall short of 0.95, so the low congruence\n764\tis a stable feature of the data rather than a small-sample artifact, consistent with the exploratory\n765\tstatus of the comparison.\n766\tFig 5 shows why the congruence values are so low. In Japan, sweet, happy, and hot load\n767\ttogether on a positive factor, while negative affect clusters with sour, and salty groups with bitter\n768\tand surprise. In Argentina, salty, sweet, and happy collapse into a warm–pleasant factor, while\n769\tanger, disgust, fear, sour, and bitter load together on a negative-affect factor, with cold defining\n770\ta separate third dimension, and hot and surprise loading on both of the first two factors rather\n771\tthan on either alone. In Italy, sweet is nearly a single-item factor, while salty, happy, and surprise\n772\tform a separate dimension and negative affect loads with sour, bitter, and cold. These are different\n773\tsemantic organizations of the same descriptor set, not small rotations of a common structure: in\n774\tArgentina the organizing principle is affective valence, in Italy it is closer to the tastes themselves.\n775\tTable 3. Mean diagonal Tucker congruence coefficients between the three cohort-specific factor solutions\n776\t(three-factor truncation, oblimin rotation, maximum-likelihood extraction). Values above 0.95 are usually\n777\tinterpreted as factor equivalence and values in [0.85, 0.95) as fair similarity; every off-diagonal value here falls\n778\tbelow both thresholds.\n779\t\n780\tJapan\n781\tArgentina\n782\tItaly\n783\t\n784\tJapan\n785\t\n786\tArgentina\n787\t\n788\tItaly\n789\t\n790\t1.000\n791\t0.613\n792\t0.777\n793\t\n794\t0.613\n795\t1.000\n796\t0.460\n797\t\n798\t0.777\n799\t0.460\n800\t1.000\n801\t\n802\t13\n803\t\n804\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n805\t\n806\tA preprint\n807\t\n808\tFig 5. Three-factor loading patterns by cohort. Blue indicates positive loadings and red negative. The\n809\tdashed-outline cell in the Italian solution marks a Heywood case (loading ≈ 1.00, residual variance close to\n810\tzero), marking the Italian three-factor solution as exploratory.\n811\t\n812\t3.7\n813\t\n814\tAcoustic characterization of the stimuli\n815\t\n816\tEverything so far has treated the clips as fixed and the listeners as the variable of interest. This last\n817\tsection reverses the perspective and asks what the clips actually sound like, since a cross-cultural\n818\tdifference is only interpretable once we know what acoustic evidence the three cohorts were working\n819\tfrom. We characterized the 100 fine-tuned clips (25 per prompt) on eight acoustic descriptors and a\n820\tPlomp–Levelt sensory-dissonance index (Section 2.4.7), read as relative patterns across prompts.\n821\tTwo results stand out (Fig 6). First, the fine-tuned model gave a distinctive acoustic signature\n822\tto only one taste. Sour clips are markedly brighter, sharper, higher-pitched, rougher, and more\n823\tarticulated than the others (centroid 741 versus 393–482 Hz; pitch 250 versus 81–127 Hz; spectral\n824\tflux 40 versus 23–30), whereas sweet, salty, and bitter collapse into a single dark, smooth, lowpitched region: in within-feature z-scored descriptor space those three lie only 0.4–1.1 apart, while\n825\tsour sits 2.9–3.2 from every one of them. Salty in particular has the lowest centroid and pitch\n826\tof any prompt and none of the crisp, percussive profile of designed salty sounds [13]. This is a\n827\tconcrete stimulus-level reason for the weak salty match seen in both tasks, and it complements the\n828\tintrinsic-ambiguity account developed in the Discussion.\n829\tSecond, although the model did not actually make its bitter-prompted clips dissonant (they are\n830\tamong the most consonant of the four prompts), sensory dissonance is nonetheless a shared route\n831\t14\n832\t\n833\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n834\t\n835\tA preprint\n836\t\n837\tto both sour and bitter perception. Across the 100 clips, the dissonance index correlates positively\n838\twith pooled perceived sour (r = .25, p = .012) and bitter (r = .30, p = .003) and negatively with\n839\tsweet (r = −.47, p < .001); spectral roughness shows the same pattern more strongly (sour r = .31,\n840\tbitter r = .41, sweet r = −.57, all p ≤ .002). Dissonance is thus a single perceptual axis that does\n841\tnot separate the two negative tastes—precisely the substrate that a sour–bitter cross-map requires.\n842\tThe prompt that generated a clip is the designer’s intention, not necessarily what listeners heard.\n843\tRelabelling each clip by its perceived dominant taste (the highest of its four response-style-corrected\n844\ttaste ratings) redistributes the prompts substantially: only 39 of the 100 clips were heard as the\n845\ttaste they were generated for (Table 4). Sweet is the only prompt reliably recovered (18/25); the\n846\tbitter prompt almost never is (2/25), its clips heard instead as sour (11) or sweet (10). Grouped by\n847\tperceived rather than intended taste, the acoustic contrasts become cleaner (Appendix A, Fig A7):\n848\tclips heard as sweet are the softest and most consonant (roughness and dissonance about 0.6 SD\n849\tbelow the grand mean), those heard as salty the most articulated and widest-band, and those heard\n850\tas sour or bitter the roughest and most dissonant, with perceived bitter the most extreme. A\n851\tk-means analysis of the perceived-taste ratings finds no clean four-way structure (best silhouette at\n852\tk = 2, 0.33 versus 0.27 at k = 4), so perceived bitter and sour share a single rough, dissonant region\n853\trather than forming separate perceptual types.\n854\tBecause each clip is rated by only about ten participants (5 to 26 across clips), this dominanttaste assignment is noisy: bootstrapping the raters reproduces the assigned taste on a median of\n855\t79% of resamples, but for nine of the 100 clips the dominant taste flips in more than half. We\n856\ttherefore treat the clip-level relabeling as exploratory rather than a stable per-clip decoding of taste.\n857\tTable 4. Intended prompt (rows) versus perceived dominant taste (columns) for the 100 fine-tuned clips.\n858\tPerceived taste is the highest of each clip’s four response-style-corrected taste ratings. Only the 39\n859\ton-diagonal clips (bold) were heard as the taste they were generated for; the bitter prompt is heard as sour\n860\tor sweet far more often than as bitter.\n861\tPrompt ↓ / Heard →\n862\tSweet\n863\tSour\n864\tSalty\n865\tBitter\n866\t\n867\tSweet\n868\t\n869\tSour\n870\t\n871\tSalty\n872\t\n873\tBitter\n874\t\n875\t18\n876\t1\n877\t6\n878\t10\n879\t\n880\t2\n881\t10\n882\t6\n883\t11\n884\t\n885\t3\n886\t8\n887\t9\n888\t2\n889\t\n890\t2\n891\t6\n892\t4\n893\t2\n894\t\n895\tDeriving perceived taste separately within each cohort brings the listener-side and stimulus-side\n896\tanalyses together, and shows that the perceptual mapping is shared for sweetness but reorganized\n897\tfor the negative tastes (Fig 7). Clips heard as sweet have the same soft, consonant profile everywhere\n898\t(cross-cohort r = 0.93–0.99; roughness predicts reduced perceived sweetness in every cohort,\n899\tr = −0.42 to −0.51, all p < .05). What differs is the taste descriptor attached to the rough, bright,\n900\tdissonant clips: within-cohort correlations between clip-level spectral roughness and the perceived\n901\ttaste ratings tie that region to sourness in Italy (roughness–sour r = .36, p < .05; roughness–bitter\n902\tr = .07), to bitterness in Argentina (r = .07 and r = .40, p < .05), and to both in Japan (r = .30\n903\tand r = .29, both p < .05; full table in Appendix A, Table A20). The Argentine bitter clips are\n904\tthe brightest and roughest of all (≈ 0.7 SD above the mean), the very profile Italian and Japanese\n905\tlisteners assign to sourness, and Japanese listeners almost never choose bitter as the dominant\n906\tdescriptor (2 of 99 clips, against 44 heard as sour). The per-cohort acoustic profiles of perceived\n907\tsweet are thus nearly identical, whereas those of perceived sour and bitter are uncorrelated or\n908\tnegatively correlated across cohorts. The sour–bitter swap and the diffuse, sour-centred Japanese\n909\tmap seen in the rating structure are therefore confirmed in the stimulus acoustics: the three cohorts\n910\t15\n911\t\n912\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n913\t\n914\tA preprint\n915\t\n916\tFig 6. Acoustic descriptors of the 100 fine-tuned clips by intended taste prompt (white circle marks the\n917\tmean). Sour is the lone acoustic outlier; sweet, salty, and bitter occupy a common dark, smooth, low-pitched\n918\tregion. Tempo and zero-crossing rate are essentially non-discriminating across prompts.\n919\t\n920\twere listening to the same sounds and attaching different taste descriptors to them.\n921\t\n922\t16\n923\t\n924\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n925\t\n926\tA preprint\n927\t\n928\tFig 7. Acoustic z-profile (relative to all 100 clips) of each perceived taste, derived separately within each\n929\tcohort. Clips heard as sweet are soft and consonant in every cohort; the rough, bright, dissonant region is\n930\theard as sour in Italy and Japan but as bitter in Argentina, and Japanese listeners rarely assign bitter at all.\n931\tBlue marks features above the cross-clip average, red below.\n932\t\n933\t4\n934\t\n935\tDiscussion\n936\t\n937\tThe present study examined whether AI-mediated auditory–gustatory correspondences generalize\n938\tacross cultures and, if not, what mechanisms account for the observed differences. The results\n939\t17\n940\t\n941\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n942\t\n943\tA preprint\n944\t\n945\tindicate that they do not generalize uniformly. Cross-cultural variation emerged less as a simple\n946\tshift in overall ratings than as differences in how listeners organized the relationship between musical\n947\tstimuli and taste descriptors. Two complementary processes appear to underlie this pattern. First,\n948\tculturally specific response styles influenced the absolute level of semantic ratings. Second, even\n949\tafter accounting for these response-style differences, the semantic organization of auditory–gustatory\n950\tcorrespondences remained different across countries.\n951\tAt the preference level, the findings provide a partial replication of the original Italian study.\n952\tParticipants from Italy and Argentina consistently preferred music generated by the fine-tuned\n953\tMusicGen model over the original model, whereas Japanese participants did not. This pattern\n954\tsuggests that the benefits of fine-tuning are not equally transferable across listening cultures. By\n955\tcontrast, the salty prompt performed poorly in all three cohorts, indicating a limitation of the\n956\tgenerated stimuli rather than a purely cultural effect.\n957\tThe cross-cultural comparison also refines the conclusions of the original Italian study. Spanio et\n958\tal. [12] demonstrated that a fine-tuned generative model could produce musical stimuli perceived as\n959\tmore coherent with taste prompts than those generated by the original model. The present findings\n960\tshow that this conclusion depends partly on cultural context. Although the overall advantage of the\n961\tfine-tuned model remains evident in two of the three countries, the comparison reveals substantial\n962\theterogeneity that is obscured when responses are pooled across listeners. Similarly, the clear\n963\tprompt–descriptor structure observed in the original Italian sample does not remain invariant across\n964\tcultures but reorganizes in different ways across the three cohorts.\n965\tA second contribution concerns measurement. Raw semantic ratings alone would suggest large\n966\tdifferences between countries, largely because Japanese participants systematically used higher but\n967\tless extreme ratings than the Argentine and Italian cohorts. Once these response-style differences\n968\twere taken into account through within-subject normalization, the overall country effect largely\n969\tdisappeared. However, the interactions describing the relationship between prompts and semantic\n970\tdescriptors remained essentially unchanged. This distinction is important because it shows that\n971\tcross-cultural variation cannot be reduced to differences in scale use. Instead, listeners from different\n972\tcultures appear to organize the semantic relationships between music and taste differently, even\n973\tafter response-style effects are removed.\n974\tTaken together, these findings suggest that cross-cultural variation in AI-mediated sonic seasoning\n975\toperates at two distinct levels. One concerns how participants use semantic rating scales, whereas\n976\tthe other concerns how auditory information is mapped onto gustatory concepts. Distinguishing\n977\tthese two sources of variation is essential for interpreting cross-cultural evaluations of generative\n978\tmusic systems, since apparent differences between populations may reflect changes in semantic\n979\torganization rather than simple differences in response behaviour.\n980\tThe cross-cultural reorganization observed in the present study also suggests that the relationship\n981\tbetween sound and taste is mediated by multiple cognitive processes rather than by direct perceptual\n982\tassociations alone. Deng et al. [13] identified six recurrent strategies that listeners use when assigning\n983\ttaste meanings to sound: reliance on sensory features, subjective preference, personal experiential\n984\tanalogies, affective responses, somatic reactions, and cognitive abstractions such as metaphor or\n985\tinternal scaling. Among these, only the direct use of sensory features is likely to be relatively\n986\tindependent of cultural experience. The remaining strategies are inherently shaped by individual\n987\texperience and, consequently, by the musical, culinary, and linguistic environment in which listeners\n988\tare embedded.\n989\tOur findings are consistent with this interpretation. The differences observed across countries\n990\tdo not necessarily reflect fundamentally different perceptual mechanisms; rather, they suggest\n991\tthat listeners rely on different combinations of these mediating strategies when interpreting the\n992\tsame musical stimuli. Preferences, culturally familiar foods, emotional associations, and linguistic\n993\t18\n994\t\n995\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n996\t\n997\tA preprint\n998\t\n999\tcategories all contribute to how a sound is ultimately translated into a taste concept. From\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d14d750d-13e3-418b-be1b-cfe55ef8a9ec", "uuid": "1ef6ca1c-c6c7-402c-bd2c-ac2cf8c09de5", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T06:07:41.792Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper.txt", "content": "\nResults\n\nThe analyses below follow a chain of increasingly specific questions. We first verify that the three\ncohorts are comparable on background variables, and then ask whether the fine-tuned model is\npreferred over the base model in each country (Task 1). Turning to the rating task (Task 2), we\nask whether the three cohorts describe the same clips in the same way, and whether any country\ndifference reflects how participants use the rating scale or how they organize the sound-to-taste\nmapping itself. We then characterize that organization: how much listeners within a cohort agree\nwith one another, and how the twelve descriptors group into a smaller number of latent dimensions.\nFinally, we turn from the listeners to the stimuli and ask what the generated clips actually sound\nlike, and which taste descriptor each cohort attaches to a given acoustic profile. Throughout, prompt\ndenotes the taste used to condition the generative model and descriptor one of the twelve items\nparticipants rated.\n\n3.1\n\nSample composition\n\nThe pooled sample comprises 140 Japanese, 104 Argentine, and 117 Italian respondents (Table 1)\nand differs substantially on background variables: age varied by group (H(2) = 77.89, p < .001), and\nFisher’s exact tests rejected homogeneity for gender, hearing experience, and eating experience (all\np < .001 via Monte Carlo simulation). The cohorts are therefore not interchangeable on background\ncharacteristics, and these variables are carried as covariates in every rating model reported below.\nTable 1. Demographic summary by cultural group. Age is mean ± standard deviation; gender is percentage\nof the within-group sample (Other / NA pools Other and Not specified); time is the median minutes to\ncomplete the survey (median because completion time is right-skewed).\n\n3.2\n\nGroup\n\nN\n\nAge (y)\n\n% Male\n\n% Female\n\n% Other / NA\n\nTime (min)\n\nJapan\nArgentina\nItaly\n\n140\n104\n117\n\n27.2 ± 13.3\n42.4 ± 15.0\n34.2 ± 14.7\n\n28.6\n49.0\n53.8\n\n68.6\n50.0\n43.6\n\n2.9\n1.0\n2.6\n\n8.0\n8.0\n8.0\n\nPooled\n\n361\n\n33.9 ± 15.5\n\n42.7\n\n55.1\n\n2.2\n\n8.0\n\nTask 1: model preference\n\nTask 1 asks the simplest question in the study: presented with two clips generated from the same\ntaste prompt, do listeners prefer the one produced by the fine-tuned model? Preferences are placed\non a 0–10 slider with 5 as the neutral midpoint, so a participant mean above 5 favours the fine-tuned\nmodel and a mean below 5 the base model.\n8\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nThe answer is a clear partial replication. One-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the participant\nmean scores show that the fine-tuned model is preferred in Argentina (median = 5.6, rank-biserial\nr = .29, 95% CI [.08, .51], p = .006) and in Italy (median = 5.2, r = .30, [.09, .50], p = .003), but not\nin Japan (median = 5.0, r = −.15, [−.34, .05], p = .93). The within-country effect sizes are small\nin Argentina and Italy and indistinguishable from zero in Japan. Comparing the three countries\ndirectly, the participant means differ, H(2) = 11.47, p = .003, η 2 = .03; Dunn’s post-hoc tests\nattribute this to the two contrasts involving Japan, which differs from both Argentina (padj = .006)\nand Italy (padj = .006), whereas Argentina and Italy do not differ (padj = 1.00). A linear mixed\nmodel on the trial-level data, with random intercepts for participant and stimulus, reproduces this\npattern: the estimated preference above neutral is positive in Argentina (+0.43, p = .012) and Italy\n(+0.47, p = .021) and null in Japan (−0.15, p = .33), with a significant country effect (p = .003).\nThe conclusion therefore does not depend on how the nesting of trials within participants is handled.\nFig 2 shows where the advantage comes from. Within Argentina and Italy the per-prompt\nbreakdown locates the fine-tuning advantage in the sweet and sour prompts (Bonferroni-corrected\nr in [.34, .60], all padj ≤ .010), and the between-country difference is concentrated on the same\ntwo prompts (sweet H(2) = 16.45, p < .001; sour H(2) = 13.37, p = .001), where Argentine and\nItalian medians are highest and Japanese medians stay near neutral. Bitter and salty show no\nreliable cross-country difference (p = .094 and p = .216, uncorrected; full test table in Appendix A,\nTable A5). Salty is also the weakest matched prompt overall—at the neutral point in Argentina\n(r = −.16) and below neutral in Italy (r = −.46) and Japan (r = −.35). Because it fails in every\ncohort alike, this points to a limitation of the fine-tuned model’s salty stimuli rather than to a\ncountry-specific failure, a reading we return to when we examine the acoustics of the clips themselves.\n\nFig 2. Model preference summaries by prompt and cultural group, shown as one panel per cohort. Points\nmark the median of the participant mean scores, vertical bars show bootstrap 95% confidence intervals, and\nthe dashed horizontal line marks the neutral point of 5. Scores in the shaded band (above 5) favor the\nfine-tuned model, those below favor the base model.\n\n3.3\n\nTask 2: perception ratings\n\nTask 1 establishes that the three cohorts reach different verdicts on the model. Task 2 asks how\nthey hear the clips, by having every participant rate the same fine-tuned excerpts on all twelve\n\n9\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\ndescriptors. The question is whether the three-dimensional pattern of ratings—country by prompt\nby descriptor—has the same shape everywhere.\nThe left half of Table 2 reports the omnibus Type III ANOVA on the raw ratings. The largest\nterm is the main effect of group (F (2, 11,768) = 436.22, p < .001), that is, a difference in the overall\nlevel at which the three cohorts rate. The critical result, however, is the three-way group × prompt\n× descriptor interaction (F (66, 11,768) = 2.96, p < .001): this term asks whether the profile of\ndescriptor ratings across the four prompts has the same shape in all three countries, and it says\nthat it does not. The two-way group × descriptor interaction is also sizeable (F (22, 11,768) = 14.43,\np < .001), while the raw group × prompt term is small (F (6, 11,768) = 2.43, p = .024). All of\nthese cross-cultural terms are highly significant but account for little variance individually (all\ngroup-related partial η 2 ≤ .03), so they are best described as structured but modest tendencies\nrather than wholesale reorganizations of perception.\nTwo checks confirm that this structure is not an artifact of how the data were modelled or\nassembled. First, a linear mixed model with the same fixed-effects structure and crossed random\nintercepts for participant and stimulus song, (1 | participant) + (1 | song), leaves the three-way\ninteraction highly significant (p < .001) and confirms the group, prompt, descriptor, and group ×\ndescriptor effects; only the raw group × prompt interaction fails to survive (p = .428). We therefore\ntreat the three-way interaction, not the prompt-level country shift, as the stable cross-cultural\nsignal. Song-level variance is small relative to the residual (σ̂song = 0.04 versus σ̂residual = 1.01), so\nindividual songs are controlled for without driving the result. Second, retaining the eight respondents\nwith a gender other than Male or Female and the twenty-two Professional Eaters excluded by the\nmain model, or excluding the legacy Italian wave, leaves the four group-related terms essentially\nunchanged, with the group × descriptor and three-way interactions highly significant (p < .001)\nin every variant. Within the Italian cohort, collection wave has no effect on ratings (p = .81) and\nno interaction with the prompt × descriptor structure (p = .28), so the legacy (n = 90) and newly\ncollected (n = 27) Italian participants are interchangeable here; the legacy-excluded re-fit, resting\non only those 27, is best read as a directional check rather than a precise re-estimate.\nA group main effect of this size is, however, ambiguous. It could mean that Japanese listeners\ngenuinely heard more taste in the clips, or simply that they used the 1–5 scale differently from the\nother two cohorts. The next section separates these two possibilities.\n\n3.4\n\nSeparating scale use from perceptual structure\n\nResponse styles—habits of using a rating scale in a particular way regardless of item content—\ndiffer markedly between the cohorts (Fig 3). Japanese participants have a substantially higher\nacquiescence index, the mean of their 36 ratings (ARS = 2.69), than Argentine (1.98) or Italian\n(1.97) participants, but a lower extreme-response index, the proportion of ratings at either end of the\nscale (Japan = 0.26, Argentina = 0.48, Italy = 0.47); Kruskal–Wallis tests confirm both differences\n(ARS: p = 6.84 × 10−35 ; ERS: p = 8.61 × 10−18 ). In short, Japanese respondents used higher but\nmore central ratings, the Argentine and Italian groups lower and more extreme ones—exactly the\nprofile that would inflate a group main effect without any difference in what was heard.\nTo remove this component we re-expressed every rating relative to the participant’s own mean\nand spread (within-subject z-scoring), which places a participant who rates everything high on the\nsame footing as one who rates everything low. The right half of Table 2 shows the consequence,\nand it is sharp. The main effect of group disappears completely (F (2, 11,732) = 0.002, p = .998,\npartial η 2 < .001), and the group × prompt term disappears with it (F (6, 11,732) = 0.61, p = .723).\nBy contrast, group × descriptor and the three-way interaction remain strong, with effect sizes\nessentially unchanged from the raw model (partial η 2 = .028 and .018). The corresponding z-scored\n10\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nTable 2. Type III ANOVA of the Task 2 ratings, before (left) and after (right) within-subject normalization,\nwith sum-to-zero contrasts on all categorical predictors. Numerator df is the same in both models;\ndenominator df = 11,768 (raw) and 11,732 (z-scored, one participant with zero within-subject variance\nexcluded). Reading the two halves side by side isolates what response style accounts for: the group main\neffect collapses from partial η 2 = .069 to below .001 and group × prompt becomes non-significant, whereas\ngroup × descriptor and the three-way interaction are essentially unchanged. Full tables including sums of\nsquares are given in Appendix A (Tables A7 and A8).\nRaw ratings\nTerm\n\ndf\n\nGroup\nPrompt\nDescriptor\nHearing experience\nEating experience\nGender\nGroup × Prompt\nGroup × Descriptor\nPrompt × Descriptor\nGroup × Prompt × Descriptor\n\n2 436.22 < .001\n3 23.28 < .001\n11 29.64 < .001\n2 31.62 < .001\n1\n3.55\n.060\n1\n0.91\n.340\n6\n2.43\n.024\n22 14.43 < .001\n33 25.58 < .001\n66\n2.96 < .001\n\nWithin-subject z scores\n\np partial η 2\n\nF\n\n.069\n.006\n.027\n.005\n< .001\n< .001\n.001\n.026\n.067\n.016\n\nF\n0.002\n11.51\n35.44\n0.03\n0.08\n0.01\n0.61\n15.54\n28.41\n3.18\n\np partial η 2\n.998\n< .001\n< .001\n.969\n.779\n.911\n.723\n< .001\n< .001\n< .001\n\n< .001\n.003\n.032\n< .001\n< .001\n< .001\n< .001\n.028\n.074\n.018\n\nmixed model (1 | participant) + (1 | song) gives the same qualitative result (non-significant group\n× prompt, p = .714; significant three-way interaction, p < .001). Most of the raw country-level\nrating shift was therefore a scale-use difference; what remains is the country-specific structure of the\nmapping.\nThat surviving structure is visible directly in the taste ratings (Fig 4). Each panel is a promptby-descriptor grid, and a model that reproduced the intended correspondences would place its\nhighest values along the outlined diagonal. Italy comes closest, retaining a clear sweet–sour–bitter\ndiagonal with a weak salty cell. Argentina keeps sweet and salty but cross-maps sour and bitter,\nthe sour prompt pulling the bitter descriptor and the bitter prompt the sour one. Japan is the\nmost diffuse, with off-diagonal spillover around sour and no salty match. The raw ratings show the\nsame three arrangements superimposed on the country-level offset (Appendix A, Fig A1), which\nis what motivates working with the normalized values. The differences extend into the emotional\nand thermal descriptors, again not identically. In the raw ratings, sweet clips are rated happier and\nwarmer and sour and bitter clips colder and more negative, especially in Argentina and Italy, while\nthe Japanese cohort shows a uniformly elevated affective profile across both positive and negative\ndescriptors (Appendix A, Fig A2). Once that inflation is removed a structural difference remains\n(Fig A3): the sour prompt recruits negative-affect descriptors (anger, disgust, fear) most strongly in\nJapan, the bitter prompt aligns chiefly with sadness in both Japan and Italy, and the sweet prompt\naligns with happy and hot in all three cohorts but to different degrees.\n\n3.5\n\nAgreement between listeners\n\nThe heatmaps average over listeners, so a cohort could show a clean mean structure while its\nindividual members disagreed completely. Before asking how the descriptor space is organized, we\ntherefore ask how much the listeners within a cohort agree about any single clip, using the mean\npairwise distance (MPD, the average absolute difference between two raters of the same cell, 0\nindicating perfect agreement) and Krippendorff’s α (1 perfect agreement, 0 chance).\nCell-level disagreement is moderate and comparable across cohorts (mean MPD 0.27–0.29 on\n11\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nFig 3. Per-participant response-style distributions by cultural group. The left panel shows the acquiescence\nresponse style index (participant mean across ratings), and the right panel shows the extreme response style\nindex (proportion of 1s and 5s); the two panels use independent y-axes because the indices differ in unit.\nJapanese participants show the highest acquiescence but the lowest extreme response, whereas the Argentine\nand Italian cohorts use lower but more extreme ratings.\n\nFig 4. Mean within-subject z-scored taste ratings in the Task 2 ANOVA sample. Rows are the prompt used\nto generate the clip and columns the taste descriptor rated; black outlines mark the matching\nprompt–descriptor cells, where a faithful correspondence would produce the highest values.\n\n12\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\na 0–1 scale). The aggregated α values are all well below the conventional 0.667 threshold, as\nexpected for single-trial perceptual ratings of AI-generated audio by untrained listeners, but differ\nmeaningfully across cohorts: Argentina (α = 0.192) and Japan (α = 0.172) reach roughly twice the\nagreement of Italy (α = 0.103); an ordinal-metric variant falls within ±0.01 of these (cohort table in\nAppendix A, Table A16; the per-cell breakdown is archived with the data). Italy is the noisiest\nmainly because its bitter cell collapses to chance: Italian listeners rated the bitter prompt almost\nidentically on all four taste descriptors (Appendix A, Fig A1), leaving little systematic signal for\nraters to agree on. Agreement is thus low everywhere and lowest in the cohort with the cleanest\naverage diagonal, so the cross-cultural differences reported here are group-level regularities that\nemerge from noisy individual judgments rather than consensus perceptions.\n\n3.6\n\nFactor structure across cultures\n\nIf the three cohorts organize the descriptor space differently, that difference should appear not only\nin the mean ratings but in how the twelve descriptors covary. Exploratory factor analysis groups\ndescriptors that move together into a small number of latent dimensions, and Tucker’s congruence\ncoefficient measures how similar two such solutions are (1.0 identical; above 0.95 conventionally\ntreated as equivalent, [0.85, 0.95) as fair similarity).\nParallel analysis returns four factors for Japan, three for Argentina, and four for Italy. When\nthe solutions are truncated to three factors for comparison, congruence remains low: 0.777 for\nJapan–Italy, 0.613 for Japan–Argentina, and 0.460 for Argentina–Italy (Table 3), all below the\nequivalence threshold and two of the three below the lower bound of fair similarity. A participant\nbootstrap returns wide intervals whose upper bounds still fall short of 0.95, so the low congruence\nis a stable feature of the data rather than a small-sample artifact, consistent with the exploratory\nstatus of the comparison.\nFig 5 shows why the congruence values are so low. In Japan, sweet, happy, and hot load\ntogether on a positive factor, while negative affect clusters with sour, and salty groups with bitter\nand surprise. In Argentina, salty, sweet, and happy collapse into a warm–pleasant factor, while\nanger, disgust, fear, sour, and bitter load together on a negative-affect factor, with cold defining\na separate third dimension, and hot and surprise loading on both of the first two factors rather\nthan on either alone. In Italy, sweet is nearly a single-item factor, while salty, happy, and surprise\nform a separate dimension and negative affect loads with sour, bitter, and cold. These are different\nsemantic organizations of the same descriptor set, not small rotations of a common structure: in\nArgentina the organizing principle is affective valence, in Italy it is closer to the tastes themselves.\nTable 3. Mean diagonal Tucker congruence coefficients between the three cohort-specific factor solutions\n(three-factor truncation, oblimin rotation, maximum-likelihood extraction). Values above 0.95 are usually\ninterpreted as factor equivalence and values in [0.85, 0.95) as fair similarity; every off-diagonal value here falls\nbelow both thresholds.\n\nJapan\nArgentina\nItaly\n\nJapan\n\nArgentina\n\nItaly\n\n1.000\n0.613\n0.777\n\n0.613\n1.000\n0.460\n\n0.777\n0.460\n1.000\n\n13\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nFig 5. Three-factor loading patterns by cohort. Blue indicates positive loadings and red negative. The\ndashed-outline cell in the Italian solution marks a Heywood case (loading ≈ 1.00, residual variance close to\nzero), marking the Italian three-factor solution as exploratory.\n\n3.7\n\nAcoustic characterization of the stimuli\n\nEverything so far has treated the clips as fixed and the listeners as the variable of interest. This last\nsection reverses the perspective and asks what the clips actually sound like, since a cross-cultural\ndifference is only interpretable once we know what acoustic evidence the three cohorts were working\nfrom. We characterized the 100 fine-tuned clips (25 per prompt) on eight acoustic descriptors and a\nPlomp–Levelt sensory-dissonance index (Section 2.4.7), read as relative patterns across prompts.\nTwo results stand out (Fig 6). First, the fine-tuned model gave a distinctive acoustic signature\nto only one taste. Sour clips are markedly brighter, sharper, higher-pitched, rougher, and more\narticulated than the others (centroid 741 versus 393–482 Hz; pitch 250 versus 81–127 Hz; spectral\nflux 40 versus 23–30), whereas sweet, salty, and bitter collapse into a single dark, smooth, lowpitched region: in within-feature z-scored descriptor space those three lie only 0.4–1.1 apart, while\nsour sits 2.9–3.2 from every one of them. Salty in particular has the lowest centroid and pitch\nof any prompt and none of the crisp, percussive profile of designed salty sounds [13]. This is a\nconcrete stimulus-level reason for the weak salty match seen in both tasks, and it complements the\nintrinsic-ambiguity account developed in the Discussion.\nSecond, although the model did not actually make its bitter-prompted clips dissonant (they are\namong the most consonant of the four prompts), sensory dissonance is nonetheless a shared route\n14\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nto both sour and bitter perception. Across the 100 clips, the dissonance index correlates positively\nwith pooled perceived sour (r = .25, p = .012) and bitter (r = .30, p = .003) and negatively with\nsweet (r = −.47, p < .001); spectral roughness shows the same pattern more strongly (sour r = .31,\nbitter r = .41, sweet r = −.57, all p ≤ .002). Dissonance is thus a single perceptual axis that does\nnot separate the two negative tastes—precisely the substrate that a sour–bitter cross-map requires.\nThe prompt that generated a clip is the designer’s intention, not necessarily what listeners heard.\nRelabelling each clip by its perceived dominant taste (the highest of its four response-style-corrected\ntaste ratings) redistributes the prompts substantially: only 39 of the 100 clips were heard as the\ntaste they were generated for (Table 4). Sweet is the only prompt reliably recovered (18/25); the\nbitter prompt almost never is (2/25), its clips heard instead as sour (11) or sweet (10). Grouped by\nperceived rather than intended taste, the acoustic contrasts become cleaner (Appendix A, Fig A7):\nclips heard as sweet are the softest and most consonant (roughness and dissonance about 0.6 SD\nbelow the grand mean), those heard as salty the most articulated and widest-band, and those heard\nas sour or bitter the roughest and most dissonant, with perceived bitter the most extreme. A\nk-means analysis of the perceived-taste ratings finds no clean four-way structure (best silhouette at\nk = 2, 0.33 versus 0.27 at k = 4), so perceived bitter and sour share a single rough, dissonant region\nrather than forming separate perceptual types.\nBecause each clip is rated by only about ten participants (5 to 26 across clips), this dominanttaste assignment is noisy: bootstrapping the raters reproduces the assigned taste on a median of\n79% of resamples, but for nine of the 100 clips the dominant taste flips in more than half. We\ntherefore treat the clip-level relabeling as exploratory rather than a stable per-clip decoding of taste.\nTable 4. Intended prompt (rows) versus perceived dominant taste (columns) for the 100 fine-tuned clips.\nPerceived taste is the highest of each clip’s four response-style-corrected taste ratings. Only the 39\non-diagonal clips (bold) were heard as the taste they were generated for; the bitter prompt is heard as sour\nor sweet far more often than as bitter.\nPrompt ↓ / Heard →\nSweet\nSour\nSalty\nBitter\n\nSweet\n\nSour\n\nSalty\n\nBitter\n\n18\n1\n6\n10\n\n2\n10\n6\n11\n\n3\n8\n9\n2\n\n2\n6\n4\n2\n\nDeriving perceived taste separately within each cohort brings the listener-side and stimulus-side\nanalyses together, and shows that the perceptual mapping is shared for sweetness but reorganized\nfor the negative tastes (Fig 7). Clips heard as sweet have the same soft, consonant profile everywhere\n(cross-cohort r = 0.93–0.99; roughness predicts reduced perceived sweetness in every cohort,\nr = −0.42 to −0.51, all p < .05). What differs is the taste descriptor attached to the rough, bright,\ndissonant clips: within-cohort correlations between clip-level spectral roughness and the perceived\ntaste ratings tie that region to sourness in Italy (roughness–sour r = .36, p < .05; roughness–bitter\nr = .07), to bitterness in Argentina (r = .07 and r = .40, p < .05), and to both in Japan (r = .30\nand r = .29, both p < .05; full table in Appendix A, Table A20). The Argentine bitter clips are\nthe brightest and roughest of all (≈ 0.7 SD above the mean), the very profile Italian and Japanese\nlisteners assign to sourness, and Japanese listeners almost never choose bitter as the dominant\ndescriptor (2 of 99 clips, against 44 heard as sour). The per-cohort acoustic profiles of perceived\nsweet are thus nearly identical, whereas those of perceived sour and bitter are uncorrelated or\nnegatively correlated across cohorts. The sour–bitter swap and the diffuse, sour-centred Japanese\nmap seen in the rating structure are therefore confirmed in the stimulus acoustics: the three cohorts\n15\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nFig 6. Acoustic descriptors of the 100 fine-tuned clips by intended taste prompt (white circle marks the\nmean). Sour is the lone acoustic outlier; sweet, salty, and bitter occupy a common dark, smooth, low-pitched\nregion. Tempo and zero-crossing rate are essentially non-discriminating across prompts.\n\nwere listening to the same sounds and attaching different taste descriptors to them.\n\n16\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nFig 7. Acoustic z-profile (relative to all 100 clips) of each perceived taste, derived separately within each\ncohort. Clips heard as sweet are soft and consonant in every cohort; the rough, bright, dissonant region is\nheard as sour in Italy and Japan but as bitter in Argentina, and Japanese listeners rarely assign bitter at all.\nBlue marks features above the cross-clip average, red below.\n\n4\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe present study examined whether AI-mediated auditory–gustatory correspondences generalize\nacross cultures and, if not, what mechanisms account for the observed differences. The results\n17\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\nindicate that they do not generalize uniformly. Cross-cultural variation emerged less as a simple\nshift in overall ratings than as differences in how listeners organized the relationship between musical\nstimuli and taste descriptors. Two complementary processes appear to underlie this pattern. First,\nculturally specific response styles influenced the absolute level of semantic ratings. Second, even\nafter accounting for these response-style differences, the semantic organization of auditory–gustatory\ncorrespondences remained different across countries.\nAt the preference level, the findings provide a partial replication of the original Italian study.\nParticipants from Italy and Argentina consistently preferred music generated by the fine-tuned\nMusicGen model over the original model, whereas Japanese participants did not. This pattern\nsuggests that the benefits of fine-tuning are not equally transferable across listening cultures. By\ncontrast, the salty prompt performed poorly in all three cohorts, indicating a limitation of the\ngenerated stimuli rather than a purely cultural effect.\nThe cross-cultural comparison also refines the conclusions of the original Italian study. Spanio et\nal. [12] demonstrated that a fine-tuned generative model could produce musical stimuli perceived as\nmore coherent with taste prompts than those generated by the original model. The present findings\nshow that this conclusion depends partly on cultural context. Although the overall advantage of the\nfine-tuned model remains evident in two of the three countries, the comparison reveals substantial\nheterogeneity that is obscured when responses are pooled across listeners. Similarly, the clear\nprompt–descriptor structure observed in the original Italian sample does not remain invariant across\ncultures but reorganizes in different ways across the three cohorts.\nA second contribution concerns measurement. Raw semantic ratings alone would suggest large\ndifferences between countries, largely because Japanese participants systematically used higher but\nless extreme ratings than the Argentine and Italian cohorts. Once these response-style differences\nwere taken into account through within-subject normalization, the overall country effect largely\ndisappeared. However, the interactions describing the relationship between prompts and semantic\ndescriptors remained essentially unchanged. This distinction is important because it shows that\ncross-cultural variation cannot be reduced to differences in scale use. Instead, listeners from different\ncultures appear to organize the semantic relationships between music and taste differently, even\nafter response-style effects are removed.\nTaken together, these findings suggest that cross-cultural variation in AI-mediated sonic seasoning\noperates at two distinct levels. One concerns how participants use semantic rating scales, whereas\nthe other concerns how auditory information is mapped onto gustatory concepts. Distinguishing\nthese two sources of variation is essential for interpreting cross-cultural evaluations of generative\nmusic systems, since apparent differences between populations may reflect changes in semantic\norganization rather than simple differences in response behaviour.\nThe cross-cultural reorganization observed in the present study also suggests that the relationship\nbetween sound and taste is mediated by multiple cognitive processes rather than by direct perceptual\nassociations alone. Deng et al. [13] identified six recurrent strategies that listeners use when assigning\ntaste meanings to sound: reliance on sensory features, subjective preference, personal experiential\nanalogies, affective responses, somatic reactions, and cognitive abstractions such as metaphor or\ninternal scaling. Among these, only the direct use of sensory features is likely to be relatively\nindependent of cultural experience. The remaining strategies are inherently shaped by individual\nexperience and, consequently, by the musical, culinary, and linguistic environment in which listeners\nare embedded.\nOur findings are consistent with this interpretation. The differences observed across countries\ndo not necessarily reflect fundamentally different perceptual mechanisms; rather, they suggest\nthat listeners rely on different combinations of these mediating strategies when interpreting the\nsame musical stimuli. Preferences, culturally familiar foods, emotional associations, and linguistic\n18\n\n\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n\nA preprint\n\ncategories all contribute to how a sound is ultimately translated into a taste concept. From", "numLines": 600, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 3184}}}
