{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-92f843c04a1b199e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "999\tcategories all contribute to how a sound is ultimately translated into a taste concept. From\n1000\tthis perspective, the cross-cultural reorganization identified in the present study represents the\n1001\tpopulation-level expression of cognitive processes that operate at the level of individual listeners.\n1002\tAlthough Deng et al. [13] discussed this possibility, their conclusions were necessarily limited\n1003\tto a single participant population. By extending the same general framework to three culturally\n1004\tdistinct cohorts, the present study provides direct evidence that these mediating processes are\n1005\tthemselves influenced by cultural context. The following sections examine this interpretation from\n1006\tthree complementary perspectives: the perceptual substrate shared across cultures, the contribution\n1007\tof musical enculturation, and the specific characteristics of the Argentine cohort, for which directly\n1008\tcomparable taste–music research is available.\n1009\t\n1010\t4.1\n1011\t\n1012\tA shared perceptual substrate\n1013\t\n1014\tThe cross-cultural differences observed in this study rest on a perceptual organization that is\n1015\tnevertheless shared across the three cohorts. The clearest example concerns the relationship between\n1016\tsourness and bitterness. Although the two tastes were not labelled consistently across countries,\n1017\tlisteners in all three cohorts associated them with the same region of the acoustic space, characterized\n1018\tby greater roughness and sensory dissonance. The cross-cultural variation therefore concerns how\n1019\tthese acoustic cues are interpreted rather than whether or not they are perceived.\n1020\tThis interpretation is consistent with previous work on taste–music correspondences. Musical\n1021\timprovisations prompted by taste descriptors consistently associated both sourness and bitterness\n1022\twith dissonant musical structures, while distinguishing them through secondary features such as\n1023\tpitch and articulation [22]. Likewise, studies using isolated scales and chords have shown that\n1024\tmusical dissonance provides one of the principal dimensions organizing taste judgments, supporting\n1025\tthe view that sourness and bitterness occupy neighbouring regions within a common perceptual\n1026\tspace rather than being defined by entirely distinct acoustic cues [34]. Our acoustic analysis extends\n1027\tthese observations to AI-generated music. Across the generated stimuli, sensory dissonance and\n1028\tspectral roughness predicted both perceived sourness and perceived bitterness, while showing the\n1029\topposite relationship with sweetness. Dissonance therefore appears to provide a robust cue for\n1030\tdistinguishing pleasant from unpleasant taste qualities, but not for separating the two negative\n1031\ttastes from one another.\n1032\tThe ambiguity lies in listeners’ perception rather than in the prompt used to generate the\n1033\tstimuli. The fine-tuned MusicGen model did not consistently generate more dissonant music\n1034\tfor bitter prompts than for the other tastes. Instead, whenever roughness and dissonance were\n1035\tpresent, listeners interpreted those cues as either sourness or bitterness depending on the semantic\n1036\torganization characteristic of their cultural group. A similar discrepancy between intended and\n1037\tperceived taste was reported by Deng et al. [13], suggesting that this mismatch reflects a broader\n1038\tproperty of AI-generated sonic seasoning rather than a limitation specific to the present study.\n1039\tThe same conclusion emerges when the clips are classified according to the taste most frequently\n1040\tperceived by listeners rather than according to the prompt that generated them. Under this\n1041\trepresentation, clips perceived as sweet consistently occupied the smoothest and most consonant\n1042\tregion of the acoustic space, whereas clips perceived as sour or bitter clustered within a common\n1043\tregion characterized by greater roughness and dissonance. Rather than contradicting previous\n1044\tstudies, these results show that AI-generated stimuli reproduce the same broad auditory–gustatory\n1045\torganization previously observed with manually designed musical material.\n1046\tSaltiness followed a different pattern. It was the weakest and least consistently identified taste\n1047\tin every cohort, suggesting that this limitation is unlikely to arise solely from cultural interpretation.\n1048\tPrevious work has shown that the auditory representation of saltiness remains comparatively elusive\n1049\t19\n1050\t\n1051\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n1052\t\n1053\tA preprint\n1054\t\n1055\tand is not associated with a single dominant acoustic profile [13, 35]. Our acoustic analysis points\n1056\tto the same conclusion. Compared with the other prompts, the generated salty excerpts lacked the\n1057\tbright, crisp, and articulated acoustic features that have previously been associated with perceived\n1058\tsaltiness, leaving listeners in all three countries with relatively little acoustic evidence on which to\n1059\tbase a consistent salty judgment.\n1060\tOverall, these findings suggest that cultural differences emerge from the interpretation of shared\n1061\tperceptual cues rather than from fundamentally different auditory representations. The acoustic\n1062\tinformation conveyed by the generated music remains broadly similar across cultures, whereas\n1063\tthe semantic labels attached to that information are reorganized according to culturally acquired\n1064\tpatterns of interpretation. The next section considers how musical enculturation may contribute to\n1065\tthis reorganization.\n1066\t\n1067\t4.2\n1068\t\n1069\tA musical-culture perspective\n1070\t\n1071\tThe shared perceptual substrate identified above does not imply that listeners attach the same\n1072\tmeaning to identical musical cues. Instead, the present findings suggest that cultural variation\n1073\temerges when those cues are interpreted and translated into semantic categories. Musical enculturation provides a natural explanation for this process. Listeners acquire expectations about harmony,\n1074\ttimbre, rhythm, and expressive meaning through prolonged exposure to the musical traditions of\n1075\ttheir own culture. These expectations influence both emotional responses to music and the semantic\n1076\tcategories listeners use to describe what they hear [8–10, 36]. Consequently, the same acoustic\n1077\tfeatures may evoke similar low-level perceptual responses while being interpreted differently across\n1078\tcultures. This interpretation fits the pattern observed here. The Italian cohort retained the clearest\n1079\tcorrespondence with the intended taste structure, whereas the Japanese and Argentine cohorts reorganized the same acoustic information in different ways. Rather than reflecting different perceptual\n1080\tmechanisms, these differences are more plausibly understood as culturally specific interpretations of\n1081\ta common perceptual substrate.\n1082\t\n1083\t4.3\n1084\t\n1085\tInterpreting the Argentine taste–music map\n1086\t\n1087\tThe Argentine cohort deserves particular attention because previous studies have examined taste–\n1088\tmusic correspondences within the same cultural context, providing an opportunity to compare\n1089\tthe present findings with an existing behavioural literature. The most distinctive result was the\n1090\tsystematic sour–bitter cross-map. Unlike the Italian and Japanese cohorts, the bright, rough, and\n1091\tdissonant musical excerpts that were predominantly perceived as sour in those countries were more\n1092\toften judged as bitter by Argentine listeners.\n1093\tThis pattern is not entirely consistent with previous behavioural work. Musical improvisations\n1094\tprompted by taste descriptors showed that Argentine musicians reliably distinguished sourness from\n1095\tbitterness, assigning different musical characteristics and emotional associations to the two tastes [22].\n1096\tThe present findings therefore do not suggest that the underlying perceptual substrate differs in\n1097\tArgentina. Rather, they indicate that the same acoustic information is interpreted differently when\n1098\tlisteners assign taste descriptors to AI-generated music.\n1099\tOne possible explanation concerns the acoustic properties of the generated stimuli themselves. As\n1100\tdiscussed in Section 3.7, the fine-tuned MusicGen model did not produce a clear acoustic separation\n1101\tbetween the sour and bitter prompts. If both prompts occupy a common rough and dissonant\n1102\tregion of the acoustic space, listeners may first identify a generic unpleasant auditory quality before\n1103\tselecting the taste descriptor that best matches their own semantic representation. The Argentine\n1104\tsour–bitter cross-map may therefore reflect an interaction between stimulus ambiguity and culturally\n1105\t20\n1106\t\n1107\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n1108\t\n1109\tA preprint\n1110\t\n1111\tspecific semantic organization rather than either factor in isolation.\n1112\tThe factor analysis reinforces this interpretation. Unlike the Italian and Japanese solutions,\n1113\tthe Argentine factor structure was organized primarily around affective dimensions rather than\n1114\tindividual tastes. Sourness and bitterness loaded together with anger, disgust, and fear, whereas\n1115\tsweetness and saltiness clustered with happiness (Fig. 5). A similar organization has been reported\n1116\tpreviously in studies conducted with Argentine participants. In musical improvisations, bitter music\n1117\twas associated with pain and sadness, whereas sour music evoked unpleasantness, fear, and cruelty,\n1118\twhile saltiness was linked to joyful emotional content [22]. Likewise, Galmarini et al. [37] showed\n1119\tthat background music influenced the emotional experience of coffee more strongly than its perceived\n1120\ttaste, suggesting that emotional transfer may play a particularly prominent role in this cultural\n1121\tcontext.\n1122\tLanguage may contribute to the same pattern. Taste descriptors are not emotionally neutral\n1123\tlabels but concepts closely linked to affective meaning [38]. Moreover, the semantic associations\n1124\tevoked by these concepts vary across languages and cultures rather than following a single universal\n1125\torganization [5]. Because participants completed the questionnaire in their native language, part of\n1126\tthe observed reorganization may reflect differences in the semantic representation of taste concepts\n1127\tthemselves. This interpretation is consistent with the cognitive abstraction strategy described by\n1128\tDeng et al. [13], whereby listeners rely not only on acoustic information but also on culturally\n1129\tacquired semantic knowledge when assigning taste descriptors to sound.\n1130\tThe dual loading of the descriptors hot and surprise provides an additional indication that\n1131\taffective meaning contributes to the Argentine semantic structure. Both descriptors loaded on more\n1132\tthan one factor, suggesting that they cannot be interpreted as belonging to a single perceptual\n1133\tdimension. One possible explanation is that these concepts evoke multiple culturally meaningful\n1134\tassociations. For example, hot may simultaneously reflect the contemplative warmth associated with\n1135\tthe mate amargo tradition and the convivial atmosphere surrounding the asado. Likewise, the dual\n1136\tloading of surprise is compatible with the bittersweet emotional character often associated with\n1137\ttango and Argentine folklore, where positive and negative emotions frequently coexist rather than\n1138\topposing one another [22]. These interpretations remain speculative, but they provide culturally\n1139\tgrounded hypotheses that are consistent with the semantic organization observed in the present\n1140\tdata.\n1141\t\n1142\t4.4\n1143\t\n1144\tImplications and limitations\n1145\t\n1146\tThe present findings have implications for both the evaluation and development of generative\n1147\tmusic systems. A model that performs well within one cultural context should not be assumed\n1148\tto generalize simply because its prompts are linguistically interpretable or its outputs appear\n1149\tperceptually coherent to a single population. Cross-cultural validation should distinguish genuine\n1150\tdifferences in perceptual organization from differences in response style, and should account for\n1151\tthe repeated-measures structure of semantic rating data. More broadly, the results reinforce the\n1152\tneed for culturally diverse training and evaluation datasets in generative music models, rather than\n1153\tassuming that a single cultural benchmark provides adequate validation [15].\n1154\tSeveral limitations should nevertheless be acknowledged. First, although the three cohorts\n1155\twere selected to maximize cultural diversity, they differed in demographic characteristics, and\n1156\tnot all between-country differences can therefore be interpreted as purely cultural. Second, the\n1157\tresponse-style correction adopted here was intentionally simple. More comprehensive approaches\n1158\tbased on measurement invariance or latent-variable modelling would provide a stronger assessment\n1159\tof cross-cultural comparability. Third, the factor analysis was exploratory, and the mixed-effects\n1160\tmodels included random intercepts but not random slopes. The resulting factor structures should\n1161\t21\n1162\t\n1163\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n1164\t\n1165\tA preprint\n1166\t\n1167\ttherefore be interpreted as evidence of cross-cultural organization rather than definitive measurement\n1168\tmodels.\n1169\tFourth, the stimulus-generation pipeline was anchored in English: MusicGen was conditioned\n1170\ton English taste prompts through its English-trained T5 text encoder. Because the identical clips\n1171\twere presented to all three cohorts, this generation-prompt language was constant across countries\n1172\tand cannot, on its own, explain why the fine-tuned model was preferred in Italy and Argentina\n1173\tbut not in Japan. The bias is also narrow. It is architectural, limited to the text-conditioning\n1174\tinterface: the model’s language representation is skewed toward English, whereas its learned audio\n1175\trepresentation (the space of music it can encode and generate) shows no evident incompleteness\n1176\tor comparable bias, even though its training distribution is weighted toward Western music [15].\n1177\tThis leaves only the question of how each English taste prompt is mapped onto a conditioning\n1178\tvector. Studies of large language models show that prompt language can shift a model’s outputs and\n1179\tactivate language-specific, often culturally specific, knowledge [39], and that the size and direction\n1180\tof the effect depend on the language involved [40, 41]. Because these studies concern question\n1181\tanswering, inference, and text generation rather than text-to-music synthesis, the parallel holds\n1182\tonly by analogy. Subject to that caveat, English prompting may have nudged the taste-to-music\n1183\tmapping toward a Western construal of the four taste prompts. Any such effect is a shared property\n1184\tof the stimuli, not a cohort-specific measurement artifact, and would produce a difference between\n1185\tcohorts only through its interaction with listeners’ culturally acquired semantic organization. We\n1186\tadvance this as a hypothesis, not a demonstrated cause. It is distinct from, though complementary\n1187\tto, the effect of the native-language questionnaire on participants’ own responses, which we discuss\n1188\t(with the closer linguistic proximity of Italian and Spanish to the taste concepts) in Section 4.3.\n1189\tFinally, the sour–bitter reorganization observed in the Argentine cohort should not be interpreted\n1190\tas evidence of a purely cultural effect independent of the stimuli themselves. The acoustic distinction\n1191\tbetween the sour and bitter prompts generated by the fine-tuned model was limited, and this\n1192\tambiguity almost certainly contributed to the observed cross-map. At the same time, the cohortspecific perceived-taste analysis (Section 3.7) showed that the same bright, rough, and dissonant\n1193\tclips were consistently interpreted as sour in Italy and Japan but as bitter in Argentina. Holding the\n1194\tacoustic material essentially constant while the semantic label changes strongly suggests that the\n1195\tobserved reorganization reflects an interaction between stimulus ambiguity and culturally specific\n1196\tinterpretation rather than either mechanism alone.\n1197\t\n1198\t5\n1199\t\n1200\tConclusion\n1201\t\n1202\tThis study provides the first cross-cultural evaluation of AI-mediated sonic seasoning across Argentina,\n1203\tItaly, and Japan. Although the fine-tuned MusicGen model generalized successfully to two of the\n1204\tthree cohorts, its performance was not culturally invariant. More importantly, the cross-cultural\n1205\tdifferences observed here extended beyond overall model preference. Once response-style effects were\n1206\ttaken into account, listeners from different countries continued to organize the semantic relationships\n1207\tbetween music and taste in distinct ways.\n1208\tThese findings suggest that auditory–gustatory correspondences combine shared perceptual\n1209\tregularities with culturally specific semantic interpretation. AI-generated music therefore appears\n1210\tcapable of reproducing broad crossmodal associations, but not necessarily the way those associations\n1211\tare organized within different cultural contexts.\n1212\tFrom a methodological perspective, the results also show that cross-cultural evaluations of\n1213\tgenerative models should distinguish response styles from perceptual organization. Without this\n1214\tdistinction, differences in semantic ratings may overestimate cultural divergence or obscure genuinely\n1215\t22\n1216\t\n1217\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n1218\t\n1219\tA preprint\n1220\t\n1221\tshared perceptual structure.\n1222\tMore broadly, the present study illustrates how generative AI can serve not only as a creative\n1223\ttechnology but also as a tool for investigating multisensory perception. Evaluating AI-generated\n1224\tstimuli across culturally diverse populations offers a promising approach for understanding both the\n1225\tgenerality and the cultural specificity of human crossmodal correspondences.\n1226\t\n1227\tAcknowledgments\n1228\tThis work was partially funded by the European Union – NextGenerationEU, under the National\n1229\tRecovery and Resilience Plan (PNRR).\n1230\t\n1231\tData and code availability\n1232\tThe survey data, the audio stimuli and the complete analysis code are openly available. Code: https:\n1233\t//github.com/matteospanio/multimodal-survey-analysis. Data and stimuli: 10.5281/zenodo.20841611 (CC BY 4.0).\n1234\t\n1235\t23\n1236\t\n1237\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n1238\t\n1239\tA preprint\n1240\t\n1241\tReferences\n1242\t[1] Charles Spence. Sonic seasoning and other multisensory influences on the coffee drinking\n1243\texperience. Frontiers in Computer Science, 3:644054, 2021.\n1244\t[2] Francis Reinoso-Carvalho, Louise H. Gunn, Eva ter Horst, and Charles Spence. Blending\n1245\temotions and cross-modality in sonic seasoning: Towards greater applicability in the design of\n1246\tmultisensory food experiences. Foods, 9(12):1876, 2020.\n1247\t[3] Diana Guedes, Margarida V. Garrido, Elsa Lamy, and Marília Prada. Disentangling crossmodality and affect in sonic seasoning: The effect of music associated with different degrees\n1248\tof sweetness and valence on food perception. International Journal of Gastronomy and Food\n1249\tScience, 35:100879, 2024.\n1250\t[4] Charles Spence. Exploring group differences in the crossmodal correspondences. Multisensory\n1251\tResearch, 35(5–6):495–536, 2022.\n1252\t[5] Xiaoang Wan, Andy T. Woods, Jasper J. F. van den Bosch, Kirsten J. McKenzie, Carlos\n1253\tVelasco, and Charles Spence. Cross-cultural differences in crossmodal correspondences between\n1254\tbasic tastes and visual features. Frontiers in Psychology, 5:1365, 2014.\n1255\t[6] Carlos Velasco, Andy T. Woods, Olivia Petit, Adrian D. Cheok, and Charles Spence. Crossmodal\n1256\tcorrespondences between taste and shape, and their implications for product packaging: A\n1257\treview. Food Quality and Preference, 52:17–26, 2016.\n1258\t[7] Nicola Di Stefano, Alessandro Ansani, Andrea Schiavio, Suvi Saarikallio, Petri Toiviainen,\n1259\tElvira Brattico, and Charles Spence. Consonance shapes the multisensory and emotional\n1260\tmappings of musical intervals across english- and mandarin-speakers. Annals of the New York\n1261\tAcademy of Sciences, 1561(1):e70337, 2026.\n1262\t[8] Nicola Di Stefano, Alessandro Ansani, Andrea Schiavio, and Charles Spence. Prokofiev was\n1263\t(almost) right: A cross-cultural investigation of auditory-conceptual associations in peter and\n1264\tthe wolf. Psychonomic Bulletin & Review, 31(4):1735–1744, 2024.\n1265\t[9] Marjorie G. Li, Kirk N. Olsen, and William Forde Thompson. Cross-cultural biases of emotion\n1266\tperception in music. Brain Sciences, 15(5):477, 2025.\n1267\t[10] Laura-Lee Balkwill, William Forde Thompson, and Rieko Matsunaga. Recognition of emotion in\n1268\tjapanese, western, and hindustani music by japanese listeners. Japanese Psychological Research,\n1269\t46(4):337–349, 2004.\n1270\t[11] Danni Peng-Li, Derek V. Byrne, Raymond C. K. Chan, and Qian Janice Wang. The influence of\n1271\ttaste-congruent soundtracks on visual attention and food choice: A cross-cultural eye-tracking\n1272\tstudy in chinese and danish consumers. Food Quality and Preference, 85:103962, 2020.\n1273\t[12] Matteo Spanio, Massimiliano Zampini, Antonio Roda, and Franco Pierucci. A multimodal\n1274\tsymphony: integrating taste and sound through generative ai. Frontiers in Computer Science,\n1275\t7, 2025.\n1276\t[13] Jialin Deng, Min Susan Li, Zhuzhi Fan, Hongyue Wang, Peter Bennett, Ziqi Fang, Priscilla Y.\n1277\tLo, and Oussama Metatla. Composing for the palate: Designing and investigating tastematching sounds for cross-sensory interaction. In Proceedings of the 2026 Designing Interactive\n1278\t24\n1279\t\n1280\t\fTaste–sound correspondences in AI-generated music\n1281\t\n1282\tA preprint\n1283\t\n1284\tSystems Conference, DIS ’26, pages 3038–3059, New York, NY, USA, 2026. Association for\n1285\tComputing Machinery.\n1286\t[14] Matteo Spanio. Towards emotionally aware ai: Challenges and opportunities in the evolution of\n1287\tmultimodal generative models. In Proceedings of the AIxIA Doctoral Consortium 2024 co-located\n1288\twith the 23nd International Conference of the Italian Association for Artificial Intelligence\n1289\t(AIxIA 2024), CEUR Workshop Proceedings, pages 1–6. http://CEUR-WS.org, 2024.\n1290\t[15] Angelos-Nikolaos Kanatas, Charilaos Papaioannou, and Alexandros Potamianos. Culturemert:\n1291\tContinual pre-training for cross-cultural music representation learning. In Proceedings of the\n1292\t26th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference, pages 1–10, 2025.\n1293\t[16] Anne-Wil Harzing. Response styles in cross-national survey research: A 26-country study.\n1294\tInternational Journal of Cross Cultural Management, 6(2):243–266, 2006.\n1295\t[17] Ronald Fischer. Standardization to account for cross-cultural response bias: A classification\n1296\tof score adjustment procedures and review of research in JCCP. Journal of Cross-Cultural\n1297\tPsychology, 35(3):263–282, 2004.\n1298\t[18] Jia He and Fons van de Vijver. Bias and equivalence in cross-cultural research. Online Readings\n1299\tin Psychology and Culture, 2(2), 2013.\n1300\t[19] UNESCO. Italian cooking, between sustainability and biocultural diversity. https://ich.\n1301\tunesco.org/en/RL/italian-cooking-between-sustainability-and-biocultural-diversity-02093,\n1302\t2025. Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. Accessed: 2026-07-08.\n1303\t\n1304\t[20] UNESCO.\n1305\tWashoku,\n1306\ttraditional dietary cultures of the japanese,\n1307\tnotably for the celebration of new year.\n1308\thttps://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/\n1309\twashoku-traditional-dietary-cultures-of-the-japanese-notably-for-the-celebration-of-new-ye\n1310\t2013. Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. Accessed: 2026-07-08.\n1311\t[21] Facundo Cuffia, Edgar Rojas-Rivas, Ayelen Urbine, and Jazmin Zaragoza-Alonso. Using the\n1312\tfree listing technique to study consumers’ representations of traditional gastronomy in argentina.\n1313\tJournal of Ethnic Foods, 10(1):1–13, 2023.\n1314\t[22] Bruno Mesz, Marcos A. Trevisan, and Mariano Sigman. The taste of music. Perception,\n1315\t40(2):209–219, 2011.\n1316\t[23] Gijsbert Stoet. PsyToolkit: A novel web-based method for running online questionnaires and\n1317\treaction-time experiments. Teaching of Psychology, 44(1):24–31, 2017.\n1318\t[24] Jade Copet, Felix Kreuk, Itai Gat, Tal Remez, David Kant, Gabriel Synnaeve, Yossi Adi, and\n1319\tAlexandre Défossez. Simple and controllable music generation. Advances in Neural Information\n1320\tProcessing Systems, 36, 2024.\n1321\t[25] R Core Team. R: A Language and Environment for Statistical Computing. R Foundation for\n1322\tStatistical Computing, Vienna, Austria, 2025.\n1323\t[26] Olive Jean Dunn. Multiple comparisons using rank sums. Technometrics, 6(3):241–252, 1964.\n1324\t[27] Gene V. Glass, Percy D. Peckham, and James R. Sanders. Consequences of failure to meet\n1325\tassu